00:00:26.135 --> 00:00:28.010
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So my
name is Chris Walley.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.100
So I'm an associate
professor of anthropology

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.300
here in the
anthropology program.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.670
So I think we're just going to
start out doing introductions.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.575
Mostly my work as
an anthropologist

00:00:39.575 --> 00:00:42.200
is in other areas other than in
documentary film, although I've

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.440
been working on documentary
film off and on for a number

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.650
of years, along
with Chris Boebel.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.650
And we've currently been working
on a documentary project called

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.950
Exit Zero, which is about
a community in Southeast

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.347
Chicago that used to be
a steel mill community,

00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:58.430
and all the industry left.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.130
And it's sort of looking at
the social transformation

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:01.980
of this region.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.340
So actually, a little
later in the class,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.460
we'll show a bit of
that and just talk a bit

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.032
about the process of
making that film for us,

00:01:10.032 --> 00:01:11.990
and some of the issues
that have been coming up

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:13.712
in the process of making it.

00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:15.920
But anyway, so Chris, you
want to introduce yourself?

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.212
CHRIS BOEBEL: I'm Chris Boebel.

00:01:17.212 --> 00:01:18.780
I'm a filmmaker.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.550
I have a pretty varied
background in documentary film,

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.180
but also fiction film,
mostly independent film.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.240
Some television, a lot
of commercial work.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.940
I currently work at MIT doing a
lot of science documentary work

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.280
and instructing in this class.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.640
And no science background.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.460
I went to NYU Film School.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.610
But I've done, both before
being at MIT and obviously

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.030
since, I've done a fair
amount of documentary focused

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.630
on issues that deal
in one way or another

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.470
with technology, nuclear power,
space travel, things like that.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.292
And this is the man
behind the camera.

00:02:00.292 --> 00:02:02.000
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I
think you should turn

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.940
the camera on yourself, Dave.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.310
There you go.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.590
CHRIS BOEBEL: Dave Stripinis
is our teaching assistant

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:14.144
who will be spending a
lot of time with you,

00:02:14.144 --> 00:02:16.970
both on technical
and creative issues.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.420
Really terrific editor
and cameraperson,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:29.690
and all-round guru and
confidant and mentor.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.820
Both Dave and I will
keep regular office hours

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.710
as the semester goes on.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.520
We have just a little
office across the hall here

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.780
that includes a couple
of edit stations.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.190
And we keep regular
hours in there.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.070
CHRISTINE WALLEY: That's
where all the equipment is.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.660
CHRIS BOEBEL: That's also where
you will be checking equipment

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.140
in and out of.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.470
And there are also edit
facilities available

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.060
just downstairs in--

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.860
is it building 24?

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:52.940
26?

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:53.870
The new media center.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:54.680
Some of you may--

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:54.980
CHRISTINE WALLEY: 26.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:55.730
CHRIS BOEBEL: --be
familiar with it.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:56.435
It's right downstairs.

00:02:56.435 --> 00:02:56.750
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
It's in your syllabus.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.980
CHRIS BOEBEL: But anyway,
it's in your syllabus.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.560
So that's who we are.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.790
And do we want to
find out who they are?

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.503
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, so
let's just go around the room

00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:09.170
first, if you just
want to say your name

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.000
and what your interests are.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.440
And actually, even before
we go around and introduce

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.930
yourselves, just to tell
you a little bit about how

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.000
this class works, in
case you're wondering

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.288
what you're getting into.

00:03:22.288 --> 00:03:24.080
We'll just tell you
really, really quickly,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.970
and then we're going to
jump right into things.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:26.990
And then at the
end of class, we're

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.670
going to go over the
syllabus in detail

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.170
and the different
logistical issues,

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.360
like how you check out film
equipment and other kinds

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:32.860
of things.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.970
But so basically, the
way this class works

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.212
is we meet twice a week.

00:03:36.212 --> 00:03:38.420
So we have this Wednesday
night class for three hours

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.180
and then there's
the lab on Friday.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.230
And it's a big time
commitment for students.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.998
I guess compared not to
some other classes, perhaps.

00:03:47.998 --> 00:03:49.040
CHRIS BOEBEL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.480
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But we've tried

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.980
to arrange the
class in such a way

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.320
that it doesn't add any
additional time to what

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.120
you would normally
be doing in a class.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.437
Part of the idea
with the lab is some

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.770
of what you would be doing
in the lab you would be doing

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.312
on your own anyway,
but this way, you

00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:05.462
could do it with
supervision, with help,

00:04:05.462 --> 00:04:06.920
which is really
important as people

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.860
are learning how to do stuff.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.160
So we don't presume that
people have any background

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.688
in doing video work.

00:04:12.688 --> 00:04:13.730
CHRIS BOEBEL: If you do--

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.330
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
when you go around--

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:14.390
CHRIS BOEBEL:
--that's wonderful.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:14.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY: --you
can talk about it.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.490
Yeah, if you do, that's fine.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:17.935
But even in the
last class, we had

00:04:17.935 --> 00:04:19.310
students who had
some background,

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.393
but the background varied.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:22.910
So maybe somebody had done some
photography or somebody had

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.220
done this, but then
they didn't necessarily

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.820
know about other things,
like sound or about editing

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.180
or whatever it is.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.320
So we don't presume
any kind of background.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.320
So we have the
Wednesday night class.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.667
We have the Friday class.

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:39.500
We also try, with the
Wednesday night class,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.927
we do have readings
for the class,

00:04:40.927 --> 00:04:43.010
but we've tried to keep
them relatively contained.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.135
It's kind of hard because
there's lots of readings,

00:04:45.135 --> 00:04:48.080
but we've worked very
hard to keep the reading

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.410
reasonable for the class.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.720
So again, because you're
going to be spending

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.220
a lot of time doing
production work,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:56.670
we don't want it all
just to accumulate.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:57.830
So the readings are short.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.247
A lot of the readings might
be interviews with filmmakers,

00:05:00.247 --> 00:05:00.870
for example.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.120
So we've tried to find things
that are relatively good reads.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.495
I think we have
only one graduate

00:05:04.495 --> 00:05:07.610
student, [? Catarina. ?]
So you're responsible also

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.320
for the recommended reading,
as well as the required.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.370
So the rest of you, it would
just be the required reading

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.440
that you would be expected
to do before class.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.060
And so anyway, so we've tried
to keep things contained

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.440
in terms of time commitment.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.275
Anyway, so that's just a few
of the most basic things.

00:05:28.275 --> 00:05:29.650
And then at the
end of the class,

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.010
we'll talk more, again, about
how it's actually set up

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.320
and kind of what kinds
of exercises and projects

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.768
you're expected to do over
the course of the class.

00:05:36.768 --> 00:05:39.060
And the Wednesday night class,
what we do in that class

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.398
is we watch clips from
classic documentaries that

00:05:43.398 --> 00:05:45.940
are addressing certain kinds of
issues we want to talk about.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.440
And they're really
discussion-based.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.580
So it's you responding to the
clips based on the readings,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.110
having conversations about that.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.820
And then the Friday
class is the one

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.020
that's the production class.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.380
So that's the lab part.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:05:59.250
That's where you're
really learning

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:00.292
how to use the equipment.

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:02.935
But you're also going to be
given different exercises.

00:06:02.935 --> 00:06:04.560
And we try to make
the exercises really

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.570
link up with what we've been
talking about in the Wednesday

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:07.710
class, theoretically.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.230
So for example, if in a
Wednesday class we're talking

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.480
about cinema verite-- you
might not know what that is,

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.893
that's totally fine--

00:06:13.893 --> 00:06:16.560
if we're talking about that as a
certain kind of film technique,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.518
we might send you out to
do a little assignment

00:06:18.518 --> 00:06:20.670
around MIT that's a
cinema verite technique.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.580
So we try to make the
production and the theory

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.430
part of the class always
link up and work together.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:27.218
OK?

00:06:27.218 --> 00:06:29.010
I'm sorry, you were
going to add something.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.427
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh,
I was just going

00:06:30.427 --> 00:06:33.240
to say, as far as the
way the class is set up

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.050
and what the expectations are,
again, we don't necessarily

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.450
assume that anybody has any
kind of video or production

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:40.950
experience.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:44.490
If you do, that's wonderful, and
we can work at whatever level

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:45.823
you are at.

00:06:45.823 --> 00:06:48.240
But for those of you who might
have production experience,

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.823
you know there's a
very steep learning

00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:52.410
curve to this stuff when
you start out doing it.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.240
And that's what the
purpose of the lab

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.180
is, as well, that it's a
fairly intensive time when

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.490
we can work with and get
you over a lot of the really

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.582
basic mistakes that everybody
makes when they start out.

00:07:05.582 --> 00:07:07.290
And if you've already
made those mistakes

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.060
and you're at a
further advanced level,

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.020
we can adjust to make
sure that you are working

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.160
at whatever level you're at.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:15.660
So--

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:16.860
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I'm sorry, I
keep standing in front of you.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:18.120
CHRIS BOEBEL: That's OK.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
[INAUDIBLE] anyway, OK,

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.440
so the way we're going
to do tonight's class

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.280
is the first half
of class, I'm going

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.160
to give a bit of
a general overview

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.973
and then Chris is going to give
a bit of a general overview.

00:07:31.973 --> 00:07:33.640
And then we're going
to break for a bit,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.340
and then we're
going to come back

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.160
and we're going to hop right in.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.327
And we're going to start
talking about early science

00:07:37.327 --> 00:07:39.720
documentaries in the 1890s
and watching some clips that

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.012
are really fun and interesting.

00:07:41.012 --> 00:07:43.200
Hopefully it will
be quite enjoyable.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.940
But because there's a lot to
do over the semester, which

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.160
turns out to be really short,
we're going to just hop

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.430
right in.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.260
So anyway, so just
the overview, a bit

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.395
of an overview first of some of
the major ideas in the class.

00:07:58.395 --> 00:08:00.270
So this class is about
documentary filmmaking

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.050
about science and technology,
however we want to define that,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.970
but it's also about
thinking through ideas

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.760
through the process of
making documentary films.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.480
And a lot of classes that
focus on film or video

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.375
tend to split into
here's the theory classes

00:08:15.375 --> 00:08:17.250
that analyze things and
here's the production

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.550
class that teach you how to
do the hands-on end of things.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:24.300
And this class, we've
tried to set it up

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.320
to really counter that.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.600
I mean, our idea is that
you can't separate out

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.550
the ideas from how you actually
go about making things.

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:33.659
So we want to constantly,
through the process of making,

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:36.870
be thinking about the
ideas at the same time, put

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.000
that all back together.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.250
And we would argue that, in
order to really understand

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.667
and think about
documentary films,

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:45.820
you also have to know about how
they're actually getting made.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.143
So even if you're less
interested in the production

00:08:49.143 --> 00:08:51.060
and then more interested
in just understanding

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.910
documentary filmmaking,
knowing how they're made

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.920
can completely
transform how you think

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.470
about documentary filmmaking.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.960
So that's another
benefit of the class.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.300
And just to give
one example of this

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.970
that I remember when I was
at NYU, we were both at NYU--

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:10.172
I was in anthropology, Chris
was in the film school--

00:09:10.172 --> 00:09:11.880
but when I was a
graduate student at NYU,

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.550
there was a documentary
filmmaker, Tony Buba, who came.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.410
I don't if you've
ever heard of him.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.750
He did these interesting little
documentary avant garde films.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:21.610
And he did one
called Lightning Over

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:23.610
Braddock, which is about
unemployed steelworkers

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:24.600
in Pennsylvania.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.930
And he had this--

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.430
he would have this
discussion with

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.080
some unemployed steelworkers.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.305
And one of the guys had on
his hat the Tom Cruise film--

00:09:36.305 --> 00:09:37.930
Top Gun was really
popular at the time,

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.150
so he had a Top Gun hat on.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.430
And afterwards, one
of the NYU graduate

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.680
students who was
in cinema studies,

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.278
and he had been trained to
think about film as texts,

00:09:46.278 --> 00:09:47.820
so largely thinking
about, well, what

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.990
do things mean symbolically.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.450
And so he was asking Tony
Buba afterwards, well,

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.990
what did you mean by having
this guy wear the Top Gun hat?

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:56.250
What did that symbolize?

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.960
And Tony Buba was like,
it didn't mean anything.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.668
I didn't ask the guy
to take off his hat.

00:09:59.668 --> 00:10:00.630
I mean, you know?

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.000
[INAUDIBLE]

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.940
So it is often
possible when thinking

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.783
about documentary films to
overread or overanalyze.

00:10:08.783 --> 00:10:10.950
And so what we're trying
to encourage in this class,

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.950
instead of thinking about
documentary filmmaking

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:15.060
primarily as text or
analyzing it in that way,

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.300
is thinking about documentary
filmmaking as a process.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:18.810
Right?

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:23.880
How do you go about
making documentary films?

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.320
What are the social
relationships

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.450
that are involved?

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:32.490
And we would argue that
potentially a more helpful way

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.427
of thinking about documentary
filmmaking as a process

00:10:36.427 --> 00:10:38.010
is thinking about
it in terms of being

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.830
a social negotiation
between the filmmakers,

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.100
the subjects of the documentary
films, and the audiences.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:45.600
Right?

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.270
So how do the films
come into being?

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:49.260
How do they circulate?

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.720
How are they interpreted?

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:53.610
What's the kind of
social negotiation

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.460
among all these different
kinds of players?

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.060
And there's both a documentary
filmmaker and theorist,

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.048
David MacDougall, who
argues that there's

00:11:02.048 --> 00:11:03.840
three ways to think
about this relationship

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.500
between documentary filmmakers
and their subjects, the people

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.400
that they're filming.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.740
And do you have the PowerPoint?

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.140
Do we have-- do we
have a PowerPoint?

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:13.800
Sorry.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.480
CHRIS BOEBEL: Sorry.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:16.490
We have two PowerPoints.

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:18.240
CHRISTINE WALLEY: We
have two PowerPoints.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:19.320
So the first one.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.500
So he gives this
kind of nice little--

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.500
or different ways of thinking
about this that I think

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:32.148
can be helpful.

00:11:32.148 --> 00:11:33.690
And he argues that
there's three ways

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:36.563
of thinking about this
camera/subject relationship,

00:11:36.563 --> 00:11:37.980
one he refers to
as the responsive

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.720
camera, one the reactive camera,
one the interactive camera.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.460
And he argues that
some documentary films

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.570
can have all of them
going on at the same time.

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:48.870
But what does he mean by
these, the responsive camera?

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.640
What he argues for
documentary filmmakers,

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.550
the responsive camera is
when the filmmaker is really

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:56.710
trying to respond to what's
going on with the subjects.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:58.350
So if you have a
documentary filmmaker

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:02.460
who's doing a documentary on--

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.430
it could be any kind of topic,
but that documentary filmmaker

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.012
is really trying to
figure out what's

00:12:07.012 --> 00:12:08.970
going on from the point
of view of the subjects

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.387
that they're working
with and take

00:12:10.387 --> 00:12:11.640
their cues from the subject.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.070
And maybe the pacing of the film
is going to be based on that.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.010
So for example, it
could be-- give me

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:18.480
a good example of a film.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:24.880
It could be about a
dance group, for example.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.600
So what is the documentary
filmmaker doing?

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.580
Is the documentary
filmmaker really responding

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:31.080
to what's going on
among this group,

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.420
and just sort of following
along and allowing

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.607
the pacing and the timing
of what's being shot

00:12:36.607 --> 00:12:38.940
or what gets edited later to
come out of what's going on

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.370
with that group and the
filmmaker's relationship

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:42.000
to that group?

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.680
So is the filmmaker
largely responding

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.590
to what's going on
with the subjects?

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:50.760
That's one way of thinking about
that film/subject relationship.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.470
Another way to think about
it is the interactive camera.

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:53.970
OK?

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.762
And he argues this is
when the relationship

00:12:55.762 --> 00:12:57.870
between the filmmakers
and the subjects

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:00.720
is largely about their
interaction in relationship

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:01.517
with the camera.

00:13:01.517 --> 00:13:03.600
And an example of this is
the filmmaker John Rouch

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.160
who we're going to talk
about later in class.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.640
But John Rouch films
were meant to be

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.680
collaborations
between his subjects

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.228
and himself as a filmmaker.

00:13:12.228 --> 00:13:14.520
And so he would work with
people and they would kind of

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.890
improvise topics and then he
would have-- sometimes you

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:20.320
would have young people acting
on their lives, for example.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.470
And so they were meant
to be interactive

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.760
dialogues almost between people,
where people improvised things

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.070
that ended up being
filmed so that's

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.680
a very kind of
interactive camera,

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:35.590
right, a different
kind of relationship.

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.860
And then the third one
being what he refers

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.400
to is the constructive camera.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:40.980
And that's when
the filmmaker uses

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:45.060
bits of images,
different kinds of shots,

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:48.042
and puts them together according
to a pre-established logic.

00:13:48.042 --> 00:13:49.500
So for example, if
you want to make

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.142
a film about a particular
topic, whatever it might be.

00:13:52.142 --> 00:13:54.600
And you already have an idea,
this is what I want to argue.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.850
You might go out
and look for images

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.090
that fit the argument that
you already want to make.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:04.440
And then you put the
images into your sequence

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.570
in that kind of order.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:09.000
And so David MacDougall argues
that all of them are valid.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.530
And you can make
a good documentary

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.630
based on any one of
those different modes.

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:14.047
And he argues that
some filmmakers

00:14:14.047 --> 00:14:15.838
might shift between
all of those modes even

00:14:15.838 --> 00:14:17.460
in the course of one
documentary film.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.110
But it's kind of
helpful, conceptually,

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:21.110
to think about there's
all these different kinds

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:24.120
of relationships you can have
as a filmmaker with the people

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.390
that you're working with.

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:26.970
And none of them is wrong.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.830
You can, again, do
really interesting things

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:29.925
with any one of them.

00:14:29.925 --> 00:14:34.650
But it's just helpful to
differentiate in our thinking

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:38.880
some of these different
kinds of relationships.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.060
And thinking about
documentary as a process

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.300
and not just focusing on the
films as the end product,

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:49.140
but thinking about things
in perceptual terms also

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.450
encourages us to ask
certain kinds of questions

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.380
that we might not ask otherwise.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:56.880
And we'll be talking
about this more

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.088
over the course of the class.

00:14:58.088 --> 00:15:02.400
But also thinking about
film as a process,

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.250
and thinking about these
kinds of relationships between

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.650
filmmakers, subjects,
and audiences, can also--

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.540
if you do want to make
documentary films--

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.498
can cause you to ask
certain kinds of questions

00:15:11.498 --> 00:15:14.640
as you think about the process
of making documentary films.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:16.883
So what kinds of topics
do you want to focus on?

00:15:16.883 --> 00:15:18.300
What kinds of
relationships do you

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:21.060
want to create as a
filmmaker with your subjects

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:22.260
or with your audiences?

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:25.660
What kinds of tools would you
use to focus on those topics

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.590
or to create those
kinds of relationships?

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:29.290
So when you think about
documentary filmmaking

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:31.540
as a process, you start
thinking about all those kinds

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.010
of questions, I think,
at a deeper level.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:38.375
So in this class, we're going
to be encouraging you to think

00:15:38.375 --> 00:15:39.500
about a range of questions.

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.680
And let me just throw a few out.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:45.970
One is what is this three-way
relationship between filmmaker,

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:47.020
subjects, and audiences?

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:48.310
We want to be
constantly thinking

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:50.920
about that when we watch films,
when we talk about films, when

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:52.600
you're out actually
shooting stuff,

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.730
thinking about
that relationship.

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:57.400
Secondly, what's the
larger social setting

00:15:57.400 --> 00:15:59.440
in which the documentary
films are being made?

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.530
And how does the shape the
film and how the film ends up

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:03.380
being interpreted?

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:06.830
So what's the larger social
setting when things get made?

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:08.830
Number three, what's
happening outside the frame

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.340
of the camera?

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:12.520
And David MacDougall in
an earlier book of his,

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:15.070
he has this wonderful quote
about documentary film.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:17.440
And he says that if you
think about the viewfinder

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.300
of a camera, that
it's the opposite

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.160
of the viewfinder of a gun.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.638
And what he means by that is
that if you look through--

00:16:23.638 --> 00:16:25.930
if you imagine a soldier
looking through the viewfinder

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:28.690
of a gun, the idea is to
frame an image in order

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:32.230
to annihilate whatever is
in the sights of the gun.

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:38.380
But he argues with a camera,
you aim in order to focus on--

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.830
you aim your image.

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:41.500
But the point of
doing that is not

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.120
to annihilate
what's in the frame,

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.910
but to but to preserve
whatever is in the frame.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:48.055
But in the act of preserving
what's in the frame,

00:16:48.055 --> 00:16:49.930
you annihilate everything
else outside of it.

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.340
So that's what you have.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.100
That's what remains is
what it is that you shot.

00:16:57.100 --> 00:16:58.750
But everything else,
the larger context

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:00.910
that was happening around
what was being shot

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.662
gets completely lost and
annihilated in the process.

00:17:03.662 --> 00:17:05.079
And one of the
things that we want

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:07.496
to be thinking about when we
think about documentary films

00:17:07.496 --> 00:17:09.730
is, what's all that
stuff outside the frame

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:13.030
that often we don't
end up talking about?

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:15.422
And the fourth thing
is how does the process

00:17:15.422 --> 00:17:17.130
of going about making
a documentary film?

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.838
And the kinds of
aesthetic choices people

00:17:18.838 --> 00:17:21.040
make, the kinds of
production choices,

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:24.369
how do they shape the kinds of
questions that you want to ask?

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:29.350
OK, so what ideas get conveyed?

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:31.870
How do people
interpret the film?

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:33.490
So once again, we're
basically arguing

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:36.730
that you can't separate out
the doing from the thinking,

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:39.020
that you can't separate
out the filmmaking

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:41.020
from the kinds of ideas
that you want to convey.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:42.562
And that we want to
be thinking about

00:17:42.562 --> 00:17:44.620
these all at the same time.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:49.460
And so one final thing I
just want to throw out here--

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:51.520
so in this class we think
about documentary film

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:53.050
as a construction.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:56.262
And often when people think
about documentary film,

00:17:56.262 --> 00:17:57.970
often there's a tendency
to get caught up

00:17:57.970 --> 00:17:59.500
in debates about
whether something

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:03.550
is objective or subjective,
whether the filmmaker is being

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:05.620
objective enough
or whatever it is.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.330
And we would argue
that it's more

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:10.360
helpful to think about all
documentary films as being

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.560
constructions.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:15.490
And by that, we don't mean--

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:19.870
sorry-- and by that we don't
mean that certain documentaries

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:23.905
aren't more accurate than others
because they clearly can be.

00:18:23.905 --> 00:18:25.780
Obviously some films
can be far more accurate

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:28.690
than other kinds of
documentary films.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:31.550
Some can be far more
perceptive than others.

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:34.000
Some documentary films
might be completely

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:36.550
distorting and problematic.

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.500
But saying those
kinds of things is

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:42.850
different from saying that
something is not objective

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.370
because it's saying
something's not--

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.410
if you want to say
something's objective,

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:52.300
it's implying basically,
that with the camera,

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:55.237
you can somehow just make a
record of the world and somehow

00:18:55.237 --> 00:18:57.820
not bring yourself and your own
interpretations to whatever it

00:18:57.820 --> 00:18:58.962
is that you're looking at.

00:18:58.962 --> 00:19:00.670
And is that really
possible for any of us

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:03.710
to be able to do that?

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:05.227
And so it's more
helpful, I think,

00:19:05.227 --> 00:19:07.060
if we think of it in
terms of constructions.

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:09.730
It's acknowledging that
what people do with a camera

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:12.460
is not just a
recording of the world,

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:15.443
but it's an interpretation
of the real world.

00:19:15.443 --> 00:19:17.110
That there's always
in the act of making

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.450
these things in the act of
it being a construction.

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:22.000
It's always an
interpretation of the world

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.820
out there through the choices
that documentary filmmakers

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:27.040
make, in terms of what you
choose to focus on, how

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:29.840
you choose to edit something.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:31.730
And I've heard some
people argue, well,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.460
would you put a
camera on a tripod

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.450
and just let it run, and
isn't that objective?

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:40.300
But nobody wants to sit around
and watch 12 hours of footage.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:41.830
No one will watch that.

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:45.070
In order to make anything that
anybody would want to watch,

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:49.720
you have to-- you're
telling something.

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:51.650
You want people to pay
attention to something.

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.370
So even if it's only focusing
on certain kinds of things

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:55.930
and excluding
others, you're trying

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:58.960
to get whoever your viewers are
to pay attention to something

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:01.690
and to focus on something
and not something else.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:03.670
So there's always
processes of interpretation

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:04.570
that are involved.

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:07.600
And there's always the
element of documentary films

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:09.553
being constructions.

00:20:09.553 --> 00:20:11.470
And again, we'll talk a
lot about it in films.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.482
And again, you got a
lot of documentary films

00:20:13.482 --> 00:20:15.190
that are not such good
documentary films,

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:16.390
and others that are
really great ones,

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.765
and some that are more accurate,
and others that are not.

00:20:18.765 --> 00:20:20.720
But they're all
constructions in some sense.

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:22.730
So we just want to
keep that in mind.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:26.650
CHRIS BOEBEL: So as we said at
the beginning, no experience,

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:29.380
a little experience,
even a lot of experience,

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:32.890
we can adjust the
class to match.

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:35.860
I mean, it sounds like we've
got a pretty good group with--

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:39.430
nobody has come in saying, well,
I've been in 30 film festivals.

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:41.290
So that's good.

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:45.760
And again, we're
here as a resource.

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:47.990
The equipment's
here as a resource.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:51.800
It's a really exciting thing to
start making films and videos.

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:54.160
It's very addictive,
just to warn you,

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.650
for those of you
who haven't done it.

00:20:56.650 --> 00:21:00.070
And we'll see how it goes.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.023
Yeah, go ahead.

00:21:01.023 --> 00:21:03.910
CHRISTINE WALLEY: One thing
that is a prelude to what Chris

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:04.660
is going to say--

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:06.910
one thing I forgot to mention
at the end-- in addition

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.070
to thinking about
documentary filmmaking

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:11.080
as a process, the
other end of the class,

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:16.120
I neglected to mention
the science end of it,

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:18.980
that this class is also partly
drawing on an STS perspective.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:21.588
So for those of you who aren't
a Science Technology Society.

00:21:21.588 --> 00:21:23.380
I mean you're studying
it, [? Katarina's ?]

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:25.750
in the graduate program,
history, anthropology, science,

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:27.370
technology, and society.

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:30.790
But basically, it's
a social science--

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:32.930
focus on science and technology.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:34.480
And one of the
things that STS has

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:37.360
been very helpful at offering
is thinking about science itself

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.127
as a process.

00:21:38.127 --> 00:21:39.960
How do you think about
science as a process?

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.250
So basically what this
class is trying to do

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:44.080
is think about
documentary filmmaking

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:46.510
as a process and
science as a process,

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.440
and trying to bring
them all together.

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.897
And so we're focusing on
documentaries about science.

00:21:50.897 --> 00:21:52.480
But also we're going
to be encouraging

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:55.780
you to do your final
projects about science,

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.570
engineering-related
topics, broadly defined.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:03.490
You can define that in all
different kinds of ways.

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:07.600
But we're trying to bring both
of those perspectives together.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:10.420
And just to mention very briefly
just some of the questions

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:13.360
that we're going to be
asking on that end--

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.450
is how has science been captured
in documentaries in the past?

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:17.620
What kinds of ways?

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:19.960
What kinds of narratives
get told about science?

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:23.170
What kinds of narratives might
get left out about science?

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:26.350
How have scientists themselves
use visual images and also

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.660
other kinds of media
and towards what ends?

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.250
And what's the kind
of back and forth

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:32.800
between science and other
kinds of filmmaking,

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:34.540
or between fiction
and documentary, even?

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:36.832
So for example, if we're
going to have class on science

00:22:36.832 --> 00:22:41.260
fiction, thinking about the back
and forth between documentary

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:45.430
and fiction filmmaking and
science and literature,

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:46.130
for example.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:51.010
So just wanted to
throw that out as well.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:54.490
CHRIS BOEBEL: So I'm going to
just go through and give you

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:57.490
just a quick tour
of a couple of--

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:00.460
several things that will be
thinking about in greater

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.580
detail later in the class.

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:06.970
And I take a filmmaker's
perspective on things.

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:09.760
And I should say when I say
filmmaker or making a film,

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:12.400
I'm using that
interchangeably with video,

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:16.270
because, really, the medium is--

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:18.678
people who love film would
kill me for saying this,

00:23:18.678 --> 00:23:20.470
but it's somewhat
irrelevant at this point.

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:22.682
Most films at one
time or another

00:23:22.682 --> 00:23:24.140
spend a lot of
their life on video,

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:26.057
even if they originate
on film because they're

00:23:26.057 --> 00:23:26.860
edited on video.

00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:28.270
They're even projected on video.

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:31.060
And increasingly, they're
just completely done on video,

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:34.750
even when we think
of them as films.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:38.510
And we can talk about the
distinction in greater detail.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:43.270
But just so you know, when I say
filmmaker, I mean this stuff.

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:45.820
So Chris talked
about filmmaking,

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:47.560
video making as a process.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:49.900
And I want to go through
and just talk about a couple

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:53.260
of the things that we're going
to be thinking about when

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.460
we examine that process.

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.810
So let's just start
out by thinking

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:03.460
about what's interesting,
different, or particularly

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:07.090
quote unquote "special"
about the process of making

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:08.200
visual images?

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:12.910
And obviously, film, video
is not the first medium

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:15.820
that people have used
to make visual images.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.280
I mean, from cave
painting onward,

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:20.020
people have been
making visual images.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:21.910
And other people have
been interpreting them.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:23.560
I'm sure there
were people sitting

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.180
around looking at
the cave paintings

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:29.020
and analyzing them,
and judging which

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:30.580
was more interesting,
which was more

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:34.280
representational or better.

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:36.440
So it's been around
for a long time.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:38.470
And before we
actually start talking

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:40.690
about film and
documentary films,

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:43.780
I wanted to take a quick look
at a couple of paintings.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.520
And I'm not an art
historian by any means.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:49.660
I'm not even beginning
to try to present

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:52.660
any kind of introduction to
thinking about paintings.

00:24:52.660 --> 00:24:54.160
But I picked a
couple of paintings

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:57.460
by one particular artist
that I found really, really

00:24:57.460 --> 00:25:01.810
interesting and an interesting
entre into some of the issues

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:04.600
that we're going to be
talking about in film.

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:07.240
So does anybody
recognize this painting?

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:15.530
That's better,
yeah, much better.

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:16.650
So any guesses?

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:17.450
Any ideas?

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:25.360
So it's an artist that you may
or may not know much about,

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:27.580
but you've probably almost
certainly seen his work.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:29.630
It's Hieronymus Bosch.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:34.390
And the painting is called The
Seven Deadly Sins and the Four

00:25:34.390 --> 00:25:36.550
Last Things.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:40.810
And the four small circles
in each of the corners

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:46.210
are the four last things, death,
judgment, hell, and glory.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:49.000
So you've got death up
there, you've got judgment,

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:53.230
I think, down there,
hell and glory up there.

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:58.510
And then the seven deadly sins
are the ones that we've heard.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:25:59.470
Wrath as at the bottom.

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:00.970
And then proceeding
clockwise you've

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:05.900
got envy, greed, gluttony,
sloth, lust, and pride.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:08.140
And then in the center
is the eye of God

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:10.150
who's seeing everything.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:13.450
And that's I think Jesus
rising from the tomb

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.042
in the very center.

00:26:15.042 --> 00:26:17.500
So one of the things that I
really like about this painting

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:21.160
is that it's kind of an
attempt to capture motion.

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:23.440
It looks like one of
those spinning disks,

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.390
like viewer disks.

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:35.880
So if you look at
it like this, it's

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.280
kind of telling a
story in a certain way.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.530
And you can kind
of spin it around

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:43.873
and spin it around, and so on.

00:26:43.873 --> 00:26:46.290
And I guess what that means,
which is kind of interesting,

00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:49.760
is that God is the audience.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:55.700
But what also I think is
interesting about Hieronymus

00:26:55.700 --> 00:27:00.530
Bosch's work is it says
a lot about the role

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:05.690
of the visual artist and
the way they interpret

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:08.660
the world or their subject.

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:12.448
So the first thing about
his paintings is that--

00:27:12.448 --> 00:27:14.240
and I don't know, again,
you may or may not

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:15.050
have been exposed to them.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:16.970
This is not certainly
one of his famous ones

00:27:16.970 --> 00:27:19.580
we don't actually have
a lot of his paintings

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:23.330
that we know of that
exist right now.

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:25.100
But they tend to
be very accessible.

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.630
They're very circulated.

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:29.150
Even if you don't
know that you're

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:31.985
looking at a Bosch painting,
you probably have seen them.

00:27:31.985 --> 00:27:33.860
And we're going to look
at another one that's

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:36.200
much more famous in a moment.

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:37.760
They are very striking.

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.880
They have a very, very strong
kind of view of the world.

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:45.290
And you don't really
need to a lot about him

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:48.150
or his world in order
to look at them,

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:49.880
analyze them, interpret them.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:53.300
In fact, we really don't know
much about Hieronymus Bosch

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:54.770
at all.

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:58.970
He was a painter of
religious allegories

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:03.200
and one of a school
of Flemish painters.

00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:07.280
But he's the only one
that anyone really

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.360
knows at all widely.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:12.290
I mean it was a very specific
genre that flourished

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:13.790
at a very specific time.

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:17.960
And the rest of them
are in museums or lost.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.890
And his, as you'll see,
that one in particular,

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:28.850
is extremely quoted
as a visual artifact.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.180
And I think that one of the
reasons for that, which you'll

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:35.510
see in a few minutes,
kind of interprets

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:39.230
or carries over with filmmakers
is that his view of the world

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:40.820
has a very powerful
internal logic.

00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:42.830
It's very, very specific.

00:28:42.830 --> 00:28:46.850
And it's a combination of
conventional religious allegory

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:51.110
and things that are really,
uniquely identifiable.

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:55.340
Things that are hallucinogenic,
disturbing, shocking even,

00:28:55.340 --> 00:29:01.790
nightmarish, and that is
kind of unique to him.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:05.400
And from painting to painting,
that's very recognizable.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:11.420
So he's got this body of work
that is internally consistent.

00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:13.790
And ha has a very,
very specific view

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:17.030
of the world that kind of
pushes through all of his work.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:18.860
And again and again,
you'll see that

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:21.980
with filmmakers,
particularly filmmakers

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:24.080
that we know and talk about.

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:27.470
They have a really specific
way of looking at the world.

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:30.530
And they push that view on
their audience, on others.

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:34.700
Sometimes they don't make
great mentors or instructors

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:36.740
because they have this
very, very powerful view

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.150
of the world.

00:29:38.150 --> 00:29:40.880
But it's also, if
they are successful,

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:42.180
it's very accessible.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.030
And it's and it's
very consistent.

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:46.820
If you go to see a
Martin Scorsese movie,

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:48.890
you have a feeling what
you're going to get,

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:51.445
and most of the time you're
right, even if the films are

00:29:51.445 --> 00:29:52.070
very different.

00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:55.910
In some ways, there is this kind
of very specific and internally

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:57.930
consistent view.

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:02.780
So the next painting here is
another Bosch, and it's-- oops,

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:06.590
actually this is a little
detail that kind of shows you

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:10.230
the nightmarish
melodrama that you see.

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:11.750
This is wrath.

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:18.740
And you've got these little
psychodramas is playing out.

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:21.710
Another great thing, if you're
going to be a filmmaker,

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:24.320
have melodrama,
action, violence.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:27.710
You know it always works.

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:30.920
This is the painting, though,
that you may have seen,

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:32.450
The Garden of Earthly Delights.

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:37.610
And it's a really enormous
and complicated work.

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:40.940
You've got hell,
purgatory, and paradise.

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:44.380
And it's a triptych
painting, three panels,

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:48.380
just enormous and
enormously complicated.

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:52.642
And I think it's a really
great way of introducing

00:30:52.642 --> 00:30:54.350
another concept that's
going to be really

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:59.090
central to this class,
both in terms of the films

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:01.430
we talk about and the theory
we talk about and the clips

00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:04.850
we watch, and also what
we do in our own work,

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:08.540
and how we develop
as filmmakers.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:11.480
And that is the
frame, the frame.

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:16.220
As Chris mentioned, the
question of what you frame up

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.200
in your camera is
really central,

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:20.840
because as David
MacDougall says,

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:22.273
you're making a choice there.

00:31:22.273 --> 00:31:23.690
And you're
annihilating everything

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:25.160
that's not in your frame.

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:27.980
And so what you do, what you
choose, the choice you make,

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:30.300
is really, really crucial.

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:34.970
But the frame also extends
beyond just that simple concept

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:35.730
of the frame.

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:38.000
The frame is also what
subject did you choose

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:39.260
to film in the first place?

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:41.870
What style are
you filming it in?

00:31:41.870 --> 00:31:44.270
What kind of internal
logic do you as a filmmaker

00:31:44.270 --> 00:31:46.310
bring to your subject?

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.380
What's your point of view, maybe
your political point of view

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:52.020
or your social point of view?

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:56.460
What is going on in the
social world around you

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.650
that may or may not
affect the film,

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:00.810
or may or may not appear
overtly in the film,

00:32:00.810 --> 00:32:02.650
but affects the
film in other ways?

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:07.620
So the frame is kind of
this overarching idea

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:12.030
that really defines
and determines

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:14.918
the kinds of films we all make.

00:32:14.918 --> 00:32:16.710
And the really simple
way to think about it

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:18.120
is just literally
what's in the frame.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:19.710
But that frame and
those questions

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:21.150
kind of extend beyond.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.190
What I see in this painting,
which is really, really

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:27.960
interesting, and I
think says something

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:30.630
about Hieronymus
Bosch as a painter,

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:33.333
is I see a desire to cram
everything into the frame.

00:32:33.333 --> 00:32:35.250
It's like, I don't want
to leave anything out.

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:37.140
I just want everything in there.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:41.580
And the detail just is maniacal.

00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:44.130
It just goes down to this
really extreme level.

00:32:44.130 --> 00:32:48.270
And very often, when people
look at this painting,

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:50.250
they don't look at
it like this, they

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:52.920
don't see the whole
painting, because it's often

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:54.180
shown as a detail.

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.580
The most famous panel
is hell on the right.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:58.500
You may have just seen that.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:03.420
And some people don't know that
it's part of a larger work.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.467
They just see that
panel because it's

00:33:05.467 --> 00:33:07.050
full of all this
really shocking, kind

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:08.370
of graphic imagery.

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:11.855
And it's often the rest
of it's often deleted.

00:33:11.855 --> 00:33:13.230
And if you think
about it, that's

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:15.330
kind of what you
might do with a film.

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:18.000
If you were making
a film about Bosch,

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.370
and you chose to only
focus on that panel,

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:23.430
you could just erase
completely the other two

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.140
and annihilate them.

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:28.830
And that is exactly what
MacDougall is talking about.

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:30.180
It even goes beyond that though.

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:34.530
Sometimes you just see
details of the hell panel.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:36.750
This is a classic one here.

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:41.460
See all sorts of terrible things
happening to the damned souls.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:46.080
And if you search
online for Bosch,

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.820
for his work, a lot of times
you'll get things like this.

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:51.900
So here, we're not even
seeing all of hell.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:53.550
We're just seeing
little details.

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:56.700
And again, if you
think about making

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:59.740
a film about this painting
or about his work,

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:02.280
think about what you
would put in the frame,

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:05.610
literally, like this, but
also in terms of how would

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.465
you approach it.

00:34:06.465 --> 00:34:13.010
Would it be a biographical
portrait of him

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:14.429
as a painter and a person?

00:34:14.429 --> 00:34:15.929
We don't really
know much about him,

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:17.639
so that would be difficult.

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:20.760
Would it be an academic
study of the structure

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:22.139
and the form of the painting?

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:28.750
Would it locate him historically
in his time and his era?

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:30.750
Or would it be kind of a
personal response piece

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:34.050
where you, as the filmmaker,
as another artist,

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.420
respond to his art.

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:38.610
All of those are
very, very valid ways

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:41.070
of approaching a
film about Bosch.

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:44.633
And all of them entail a
completely different frame.

00:34:44.633 --> 00:34:46.550
And all of them would
be completely different.

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:48.719
And some might be
better than others.

00:34:48.719 --> 00:34:50.409
Some might be
fairer than others.

00:34:50.409 --> 00:34:52.130
Some might be more
accurate than others.

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:56.300
But that would be more a matter
of the skill of the filmmaker

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.270
and the filmmaker's
willingness to engage

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:02.360
with the subject
in an honest way.

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:04.280
There's absolutely
nothing wrong with making

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:05.660
any one of those films.

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:08.480
So again, it's about frame.

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:10.400
It's about what you've
put in the frame,

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:15.770
and also what your mental frame
is, what your mental space is

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:18.810
vis a vis your subject.

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:23.870
So enough about painting,
something I very little about.

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:25.490
But I would just
take away, again,

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:30.740
this idea that people have
been trying to figure out

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:33.710
how to visually represent the
world for a really, really

00:35:33.710 --> 00:35:34.710
long time.

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:37.716
And sooner or later,
it always ends up--

00:35:37.716 --> 00:35:41.060
you always end up with
the basic quandary

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:46.700
of how do you take something
as big and complex as the world

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:50.330
and drill down and distill it
into something as comparatively

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:56.390
simple and feeble as an image
or a series of images or a film.

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.520
And you do that by
your these choices

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:01.140
you make as you
frame your subject.

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:08.750
So I'm going to move on
and look at a few clips.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:12.600
And these are clips that we've
picked for a couple of reasons.

00:36:12.600 --> 00:36:15.290
They're all science
documentaries, or most of them,

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:16.910
in one form or another.

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:18.950
A lot of them have
an MIT connection.

00:36:18.950 --> 00:36:23.690
But they also I think do an
interesting job of illustrating

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:25.790
different approaches,
different quote

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:28.580
unquote "frames"
of seeing the world

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:32.780
or seeing science, again
in quotes, I guess.

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:35.630
And they also--

00:36:35.630 --> 00:36:38.060
I'm not really big
on presenting kind

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:41.120
of an evolutionary perspective,
like this film superseded

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:43.490
that one and it was better
because it was more advanced.

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:47.180
But there is kind of
responsiveness, a call

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:50.150
and response to these films
as you proceed through time

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:54.800
and you see one filmmaker
responding to and sometimes

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:58.200
responding against the
work that went before.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:36:59.960
So let me show you
the first clip.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:03.860
This is a clip from
a film made here

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:11.000
at MIT by a predecessor of mine,
I guess, in the early 1950s.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:13.130
It's called Making
Electrons Count.

00:37:13.130 --> 00:37:16.190
And it's on Project
Whirlwind which

00:37:16.190 --> 00:37:19.790
was one of the very first
digital computers developed

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:23.533
by Jay Forrester here.

00:37:23.533 --> 00:37:24.200
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.930
- MIT is best known for the
scientists and engineers

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:28.070
who have studied there.

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:31.070
On the edge of the campus is
the digital computer laboratory,

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:34.220
home of Project Whirlwind.

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:37.130
This project
started in 1947 when

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:39.800
JW Forrester and
his associates began

00:37:39.800 --> 00:37:42.680
the design of an ultra
high-speed electronic digital

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:45.830
computer, which they
christened Whirlwind 1.

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:48.890
Whirlwind first
operated in 1950.

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:50.900
This laboratory's
primary concern

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:52.670
has been the development
and application

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:54.590
of reliable and
efficient computer

00:37:54.590 --> 00:37:56.480
components and systems.

00:37:56.480 --> 00:38:01.040
By 1953, Whirlwind could
perform 25,000 multiplications

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:02.580
per second.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.150
In addition to its extensive
use in research connected

00:38:05.150 --> 00:38:08.510
with our defense program,
about half of Whirlwind's time

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:10.010
has been dedicated
to the solution

00:38:10.010 --> 00:38:13.190
of scientific and engineering
problems of all kinds.

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:15.110
[END PLAYBACK]

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:17.920
CHRIS BOEBEL: OK, oops.

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:30.483
So what do you notice about
that in terms of technique?

00:38:30.483 --> 00:38:37.730
AUDIENCE: Getting the feel the
[INAUDIBLE] Was [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:40.760
CHRIS BOEBEL: Propaganda feel--
so that's a value judgment,

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:41.810
which is totally--

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:44.750
I mean I agree with
you, but why does

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.160
it have that propaganda feel.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:48.440
AUDIENCE: I think it's
just like the voice is

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:50.648
less say personal [INAUDIBLE]
things like advertising

00:38:50.648 --> 00:38:52.230
[INAUDIBLE] so it's
less [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:54.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right, yeah,
I mean it's basically

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:55.490
it's an illustrated script.

00:38:55.490 --> 00:38:57.260
How did they make that film?

00:38:57.260 --> 00:38:59.000
Somebody wrote up
what they wanted

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:01.100
to say about Project Whirlwind.

00:39:01.100 --> 00:39:03.320
And then they went
out and they filmed

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:07.912
shots that sometimes don't even
really illustrate the script.

00:39:07.912 --> 00:39:10.370
I mean they're just kind of
general, random shots of people

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:13.640
wandering around
this computer, right?

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:15.273
And they're just
filling the time.

00:39:15.273 --> 00:39:16.190
So they have a script.

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:18.740
They recorded it, no
particular voice, as you said.

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:21.980
It's just what's called
the voice of God narration.

00:39:21.980 --> 00:39:25.155
And then they went out and
they filmed x number of shots

00:39:25.155 --> 00:39:27.155
that they needed in order
to fill up that space.

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:31.910
And I unfortunately
lost my place,

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:35.460
I'm going to play
it again quickly.

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:36.260
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:37.480
- Who have studied there.

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:39.230
On the edge of the
campus is the digital--

00:39:39.230 --> 00:39:39.813
[END PLAYBACK]

00:39:39.813 --> 00:39:44.930
CHRIS BOEBEL: So static
camera, barely moving.

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:48.963
Here, whoa, a pan,
very, very daring.

00:39:48.963 --> 00:39:49.630
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:52.820
- --ultra high speed
electronic digital computer,

00:39:52.820 --> 00:39:55.220
which they christened
Whirlwind 1.

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:57.770
Whirlwind first
operated in 1950.

00:39:57.770 --> 00:40:00.393
This laboratory's
primary concern

00:40:00.393 --> 00:40:02.060
has been the development
and application

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:03.980
of reliable and
efficient computer

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:05.375
components and systems.

00:40:05.375 --> 00:40:05.990
[END PLAYBACK]

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:08.480
CHRIS BOEBEL: We see people
but we don't meet the people.

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:10.110
We don't know their names.

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:13.452
We have no idea what they think
about this or who they are

00:40:13.452 --> 00:40:14.660
or what they're really doing.

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:17.540
We know they're typing
at a typewriter.

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:19.100
Sometimes we don't
even know that

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:20.660
much about what they're doing.

00:40:20.660 --> 00:40:25.995
It's really an
illustrated text document.

00:40:25.995 --> 00:40:27.620
So the next piece
I'm going to show you

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:32.510
is a short clip from
a television program

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:35.870
from around the same
time called See It Now

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:39.740
that is also about
the same topic.

00:40:42.203 --> 00:40:42.870
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:40:42.870 --> 00:40:44.700
- The Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.740
has developed a new
one for the Navy.

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:49.800
It's a Whirlwind
electronic computer.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:51.420
With considerable
trepidation, we

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:53.880
undertake to interview
this new machine.

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:58.470
Now to MIT and the computer lab
at Cambridge, Massachusetts--

00:40:58.470 --> 00:40:59.520
Hello, New York.

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:02.760
Hello, New York,
this is Cambridge.

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:05.430
And this is the oscilloscope
of the Whirlwind

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:06.420
electronic computer.

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:12.390
I assume that like any
delicate, finely tuned piece

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:16.200
of mechanism, this has a human
element involved too, has it?

00:41:16.200 --> 00:41:17.618
- Yes, Mr. Murrow.

00:41:17.618 --> 00:41:21.100
I'm Jay Forester, director of
the digital computer laboratory

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:25.020
where Whirlwind was built.

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.570
- Well, I wonder if we could
take a look around, sir?

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:30.720
- Yes, let's first look
at one of the storage

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:34.770
tubes such as the
Whirlwind uses for memory.

00:41:34.770 --> 00:41:37.410
Whirlwind stores in
these tubes information

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:39.480
in 25 millionths of a second.

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:41.490
And further down
the row, you can

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:44.070
see some of the
electronic circuits which

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:45.150
make Whirlwind work.

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:46.380
[END PLAYBACK]

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:48.505
CHRIS BOEBEL: So what do
you notice about that one?

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:57.020
Thoughts?

00:41:57.020 --> 00:41:58.450
AUDIENCE: It's a lot--

00:41:58.450 --> 00:42:03.290
[INAUDIBLE] that takes a lot
more personal like actually

00:42:03.290 --> 00:42:06.140
get to see the face of
the person who's talking.

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:08.120
CHRIS BOEBEL: Jay
Forrester, the person

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:09.740
who's in charge--
primarily in charge

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:12.320
of development of this machine.

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:13.460
AUDIENCE: He actually did--

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:15.410
and he gives context
to what you're saying.

00:42:15.410 --> 00:42:17.750
It's not just a random
pan or something

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:19.623
like that, the environment.

00:42:19.623 --> 00:42:22.040
But he's like, oh, yeah, in
this shot, we're showing this,

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:25.990
and [INAUDIBLE]
we'll show you this.

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:27.400
CHRIS BOEBEL: Other thoughts?

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:28.775
AUDIENCE: The
sound is different.

00:42:28.775 --> 00:42:31.808
[INAUDIBLE] Like music
in the background.

00:42:31.808 --> 00:42:33.350
CHRIS BOEBEL: Generic
music, again, I

00:42:33.350 --> 00:42:36.020
mean it's just kind of like
feel like they measured out

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:36.890
the tape.

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:38.945
Like, well, we need
100 feet of music here.

00:42:38.945 --> 00:42:40.070
And then they got it, done.

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:41.540
And they cut it.

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:44.247
Yeah, I mean, this
one is-- it's drama.

00:42:44.247 --> 00:42:45.830
First of all, they're
create-- they're

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:47.360
trying to create drama, right?

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:49.280
I mean, it's the magic
of live television.

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:54.320
This is Edward R. Murrow,
this was an extremely famous

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:55.000
broadcast--

00:42:58.430 --> 00:43:00.440
extremely famous early
television program.

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:04.760
And he's bringing you
magically, magically

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:09.080
into a laboratory at MIT
to meet Jay Forrester

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:12.890
and meet the Whirlwind computer.

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:15.260
But it's still pretty
scripted, right?

00:43:15.260 --> 00:43:16.700
It's still pretty stiff.

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:25.130
And it's personality driven,
but it's also very formal.

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:27.050
It's written, obviously.

00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:29.885
I mean, he's seems to have his
lines memorized, certainly,

00:43:29.885 --> 00:43:33.350
or Edward R. Murrow has
his lines memorized.

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:37.220
But as in terms of something
that is compelling,

00:43:37.220 --> 00:43:41.240
and I mean if you can get
over the technological gap

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:43.790
between what we're used
to seeing and this,

00:43:43.790 --> 00:43:47.510
it's a lot more
compelling to a viewer

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:50.210
to watch this than to
watch the other one,

00:43:50.210 --> 00:43:54.500
which is just this very kind of
text with pictures and images.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:57.510
So I want to read a
quote from a filmmaker--

00:43:57.510 --> 00:44:02.090
a pretty famous filmmaker, film
producer-- named Robert Drew.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.070
And it's specifically
about See It Now,

00:44:04.070 --> 00:44:08.310
which was, as I said, a
popular series at the time.

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:13.880
So this is what Drew, who
was a contemporary, said.

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:15.740
"I tuned in to watch
Murrow's See It Now.

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:18.080
As the program progressed,
I turned off the sound

00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:19.430
and watched the picture.

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:21.380
The progression disintegrated.

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:23.660
What power had been there
turned to confusion.

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:24.627
The logic left.

00:44:24.627 --> 00:44:26.960
When I turned the picture off
and listened to the sound,

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:28.835
the program tracked perfectly.

00:44:28.835 --> 00:44:30.710
Later that year, Murrow's
television programs

00:44:30.710 --> 00:44:32.150
were printed in book form.

00:44:32.150 --> 00:44:35.010
They read very well."

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:40.430
So again, what he's saying is
that, although obviously it's

00:44:40.430 --> 00:44:44.900
a lot further along than,
visually and in terms

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:47.120
of drama, than the first
piece we looked at,

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:51.885
it's still really, really
audio and text-based.

00:44:51.885 --> 00:44:54.260
And if you think about it, we
just watch it without sound

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:55.885
as I got back to my
place, you wouldn't

00:44:55.885 --> 00:45:00.230
know what was going on, other
than the sign that says MIT,

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:02.345
and it's something
about a computer.

00:45:02.345 --> 00:45:07.100
You wouldn't have any kind of
sense of human drama going on

00:45:07.100 --> 00:45:11.240
or what these people
were engaged in or why.

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:16.460
And again, I personally
am not criticizing the use

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:18.680
of audio or reliance on audio.

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:21.090
But there's a strand in
filmmaking that says,

00:45:21.090 --> 00:45:23.610
well, films are about images.

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:26.240
They are visual artifacts.

00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:29.630
And you can't have a painting
about sound or painting

00:45:29.630 --> 00:45:33.230
that relies on sound where
the painting, the image

00:45:33.230 --> 00:45:37.730
is irrelevant, although that's
kind an interesting idea.

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:39.980
Now in conceptual
art, now we could.

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:42.770
But in traditional
painting, you couldn't.

00:45:42.770 --> 00:45:45.050
So why would you in film?

00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:52.190
So Drew and some other
people got very interested.

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:53.930
And they were
drawing on a number

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:59.150
of historical precursors.

00:45:59.150 --> 00:46:02.330
I got very interested in another
kind of documentary filmmaking,

00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:06.170
a documentary filmmaking that
would privilege the visual

00:46:06.170 --> 00:46:12.420
and the experiential, the
sensory, over the text,

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:15.710
over what was being said, either
in an interview or a script.

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:21.890
And we're going to show
a quick clip of one.

00:46:26.593 --> 00:46:27.260
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

