00:48:44.118 --> 00:48:46.410
It kind of gives you that
sense of just how many people

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:47.640
they were involved with.

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:49.900
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Right, I like that.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:50.670
Other thoughts?

00:48:56.150 --> 00:48:59.480
AUDIENCE: They broke apart
the different aspects

00:48:59.480 --> 00:49:01.070
of what they're
doing, handshaking,

00:49:01.070 --> 00:49:04.880
Jackie O. repeatedly smiling,
and then the people walking up,

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:07.940
kind of sizing up the
situation before they actually

00:49:07.940 --> 00:49:09.290
approached the Kennedys.

00:49:09.290 --> 00:49:11.540
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right.

00:49:11.540 --> 00:49:13.940
So in terms of the
approach, I mean

00:49:13.940 --> 00:49:16.430
you're all absolutely right.

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:19.530
It's about the experience
of being in that room,

00:49:19.530 --> 00:49:24.020
particularly for Jackie
and to some extent for JFK.

00:49:24.020 --> 00:49:26.840
This room-- you can
almost feel how hot

00:49:26.840 --> 00:49:28.400
it is, how uncomfortable it is.

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:29.480
They're on display.

00:49:29.480 --> 00:49:32.010
They're just being crowded
by all of these people.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.010
They have to stand there
for who knows how long,

00:49:34.010 --> 00:49:36.230
shaking hundreds of hands.

00:49:36.230 --> 00:49:39.380
Jackie just looks
extraordinarily uncomfortable,

00:49:39.380 --> 00:49:42.470
except when Robert Kennedy
comes through the line

00:49:42.470 --> 00:49:44.120
and then he makes the joke.

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:46.460
And she seems
relieved for a second.

00:49:46.460 --> 00:49:49.520
It's just-- it's all about
the experience of what it's

00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:54.200
like to be on display, to
be kind of parading in front

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:58.160
of the common quote
unquote voter in Wisconsin,

00:49:58.160 --> 00:49:59.307
trying to win the primary.

00:49:59.307 --> 00:50:00.890
And the whole film
is built like that.

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:03.500
There isn't any voiceover.

00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:06.470
There isn't any narration
really to speak of.

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:09.290
There are really very
few interviews, just

00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:12.380
a little kind of maybe
snippets here and there.

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:15.360
Imagine what the Edward
R. Murrow version of that

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:15.860
would be.

00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:20.060
Mr. Kennedy, how did it
feel being in that room?

00:50:20.060 --> 00:50:21.260
Well, it was kind of hot.

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:24.500
But it was very exciting to
meet the voters of Wisconsin.

00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:27.350
It would get at it in a
completely different way.

00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:30.420
This is all about
the experience.

00:50:30.420 --> 00:50:33.560
It's all about-- and it's also
about the drama in the story,

00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:35.873
this unbearable
tension that kind of

00:50:35.873 --> 00:50:37.040
keeps building and building.

00:50:37.040 --> 00:50:39.710
And then RFK breaks the tension.

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:42.800
And then it builds again.

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:45.380
And let's watch
it without sound.

00:50:45.380 --> 00:50:47.130
I mean the sound is
actually really cool.

00:50:47.130 --> 00:50:48.500
I love the sound in the piece.

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:55.100
But visually, is constructed to
tell a story without the sound.

00:50:55.100 --> 00:50:58.190
I think again, the sound
is really important.

00:50:58.190 --> 00:51:04.490
But because these long, long
shots of the handshaking

00:51:04.490 --> 00:51:09.822
and this sort of
demanding body language

00:51:09.822 --> 00:51:11.280
that everybody
wants to touch them.

00:51:11.280 --> 00:51:12.650
Everybody wants to grab them.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:21.560
Just her, stoic-- but sort of
discomfort at the whole thing.

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:24.690
It all comes across, in
the crowding of the room,

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:27.190
just the whole feel of it, it
comes across without any sound

00:51:27.190 --> 00:51:29.300
at all.

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:32.050
And I think that we're going
to actually in this class

00:51:32.050 --> 00:51:34.060
start out looking at
some silent films.

00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:36.110
And the thing that--

00:51:36.110 --> 00:51:38.680
the standard thing to
say about silent films

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:42.460
is that it's so
wonderful to look at them

00:51:42.460 --> 00:51:45.010
because they're pure
films, quote unquote.

00:51:45.010 --> 00:51:49.000
They're just visual expressions
without any kind of audio

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:51.160
or any other kind of
supporting material.

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:57.227
And the idea of these verite
films is to in some way

00:51:57.227 --> 00:51:59.560
recapture that even with
sound, even though the sound is

00:51:59.560 --> 00:52:00.670
really important.

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:03.490
As a side note, this also
is an MIT connection.

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:10.150
It was shot by Ricky Leacock,
who was the head of MIT's film

00:52:10.150 --> 00:52:11.470
program for about 20 years.

00:52:11.470 --> 00:52:13.750
And it was edited by
DA Pennebaker who's

00:52:13.750 --> 00:52:15.850
a very famous documentary
filmmaker, who

00:52:15.850 --> 00:52:18.550
was I think a mechanical
engineering undergrad major

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:19.850
at MIT.

00:52:19.850 --> 00:52:22.460
So the next piece is--

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:26.590
well, should we just pause?

00:52:26.590 --> 00:52:28.190
Let's just pause.

00:52:28.190 --> 00:52:29.060
OK.

00:52:29.060 --> 00:52:32.060
So we're going to take
about a five-minute break

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:34.370
to change tape.

00:52:34.370 --> 00:52:37.370
OK, so I want to
read another quote

00:52:37.370 --> 00:52:43.430
from another filmmaker who is
then responding to this cinema

00:52:43.430 --> 00:52:45.620
verite movement.

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:48.050
And the next filmmaker
we're going to look at

00:52:48.050 --> 00:52:49.430
is Errol Morris.

00:52:49.430 --> 00:52:51.920
Are people familiar with
Errol Morris's work?

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:53.870
Anybody?

00:52:53.870 --> 00:52:58.190
So he's a filmmaker who's had
quite a bit of popular success,

00:52:58.190 --> 00:53:00.620
at least for a
documentary filmmaker.

00:53:00.620 --> 00:53:02.990
He's done a lot
of films that have

00:53:02.990 --> 00:53:07.190
had a lot of commercial
distribution,

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:12.650
won an Academy Award, quite
a well-known filmmaker.

00:53:12.650 --> 00:53:15.290
And his approach to
documentary filmmaking

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:20.420
is completely the opposite of
the Robert Drew cinema verite

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:22.310
approach.

00:53:22.310 --> 00:53:25.550
And this is what he wrote
about that approach.

00:53:25.550 --> 00:53:27.800
"What bothers me are the
metaphysical claims that

00:53:27.800 --> 00:53:30.470
go with it, that somehow the
application of these techniques

00:53:30.470 --> 00:53:33.260
will produce truth, as
though there's metaphysical--

00:53:33.260 --> 00:53:35.163
there's this metaphysical
meat grinder,

00:53:35.163 --> 00:53:37.580
which if you put the right
ingredients in and prepare them

00:53:37.580 --> 00:53:40.670
according to some given process,
will magically produce truth.

00:53:40.670 --> 00:53:43.130
That idea has always struck
me as complete nonsense.

00:53:43.130 --> 00:53:43.910
It's a style.

00:53:43.910 --> 00:53:45.890
And style doesn't
guarantee you truth.

00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:48.110
When I was making my first
film, Gates Of Heaven--"

00:53:48.110 --> 00:53:49.557
which we'll look at later--

00:53:49.557 --> 00:53:51.890
"I would joke that I would
take all the basic principles

00:53:51.890 --> 00:53:53.780
of verite and turn
them on their head.

00:53:53.780 --> 00:53:55.800
Against that idea of
being a fly on the wall,

00:53:55.800 --> 00:53:57.675
I would make myself as
obtrusive as possible.

00:53:57.675 --> 00:53:59.633
I would put myself right
in front of the people

00:53:59.633 --> 00:54:01.220
and force them to
look at the camera.

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:03.770
I would light everything instead
of using available light.

00:54:03.770 --> 00:54:07.490
Instead of hand-held cameras,
I put everything on a tripod."

00:54:07.490 --> 00:54:10.520
So he's basically saying--

00:54:10.520 --> 00:54:15.140
he's reacting against
this kind of movement that

00:54:15.140 --> 00:54:18.260
was a very powerful movement
within documentary film

00:54:18.260 --> 00:54:22.935
and which was basically making
the assertion that we all

00:54:22.935 --> 00:54:24.560
just kind of bought
into, including me,

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:26.780
and I'm not saying
we were wrong,

00:54:26.780 --> 00:54:32.000
that this is kind of a way of
really getting into people's

00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:34.130
lives and really
representing them

00:54:34.130 --> 00:54:36.902
in a more accurate way
than some of these earlier

00:54:36.902 --> 00:54:38.360
kinds of techniques
we were talking

00:54:38.360 --> 00:54:43.250
about with the voice of God
narration and interviews

00:54:43.250 --> 00:54:44.270
and so on.

00:54:44.270 --> 00:54:47.450
Errol Morris said
no, no, no, no, no.

00:54:47.450 --> 00:54:49.940
What I'm going to do is
interview-based films.

00:54:49.940 --> 00:54:52.940
And I want to show you
clips from a couple

00:54:52.940 --> 00:54:54.590
that he's worked on.

00:54:54.590 --> 00:54:56.930
The first one is from a film
called Fast, Cheap, And Out

00:54:56.930 --> 00:54:58.880
Of Control.

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:01.250
Anybody seen it?

00:55:01.250 --> 00:55:02.990
It's a really, really
interesting film.

00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:08.510
It basically weaves the stories
of four people together.

00:55:08.510 --> 00:55:12.350
And it has this very,
very abstract point

00:55:12.350 --> 00:55:23.820
about life and chaos and death
and decay and communities.

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:29.060
One of the people in the film is
Rodney Brooks, who is in CSAIL.

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:31.220
And the clip I'm
going to show is

00:55:31.220 --> 00:55:33.624
a bit of an interview with him

00:55:33.624 --> 00:55:35.070
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:37.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:55:41.820 --> 00:55:45.870
- Evolution spent a long
time, billions of years,

00:55:45.870 --> 00:55:48.060
getting to the point
of little creatures

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:51.390
which could chase each
other around, have mobility,

00:55:51.390 --> 00:55:54.330
and interact with the
world in a meaningful way,

00:55:54.330 --> 00:55:57.810
apart from being an amoeba that
just sits there and doesn't

00:55:57.810 --> 00:55:59.950
do much.

00:55:59.950 --> 00:56:02.650
Once that stuff was there,
all this other stuff

00:56:02.650 --> 00:56:06.030
evolved very quickly.

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:08.280
Higher-level intelligence,
whatever that is,

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:13.140
is pretty easy once you have
the ability to move around,

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:15.320
hunt, chase.

00:56:15.320 --> 00:56:16.320
They're the tough parts.

00:56:25.390 --> 00:56:29.110
I don't believe it's possible to
have a disembodied intelligence

00:56:29.110 --> 00:56:31.615
without a physical
connection to reality.

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:38.270
Everything we think, everything
in our thought process

00:56:38.270 --> 00:56:41.760
is built around being
in touch with reality,

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:42.770
even the word "touch."

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:53.040
A cockroach has 30,000 hairs,
each of which is a sensor.

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:56.040
The most complex robot
we've built has 150 sensors

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.890
and it's just about killed us.

00:56:58.890 --> 00:57:03.420
We can't expect to do as well
as animals do in the world

00:57:03.420 --> 00:57:05.180
until we get past
that sensing barrier.

00:57:30.374 --> 00:57:32.390
[END PLAYBACK]

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:35.060
CHRIS BOEBEL: So the film
basically interweaves

00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:41.180
Rodney Brooks with several
other subjects, a lion tamer,

00:57:41.180 --> 00:57:44.210
someone who is a topiary
gardener who trims hedges

00:57:44.210 --> 00:57:47.450
into these very complex
animal shapes, someone

00:57:47.450 --> 00:57:54.701
who studies mole rats,
and who am I forgetting?

00:57:54.701 --> 00:57:56.490
AUDIENCE: Did you
say the lion tamer?

00:57:56.490 --> 00:57:59.080
CHRIS BOEBEL: The lion tamer,
I guess that's it, yeah.

00:57:59.080 --> 00:58:05.040
So again, it's about artifice
and nature and mimicking nature

00:58:05.040 --> 00:58:07.740
and how various organisms
live in the world

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:11.800
and how they respond to
the reality around them.

00:58:11.800 --> 00:58:14.130
So it's a very,
very abstract film.

00:58:14.130 --> 00:58:17.430
And what he's trying to do
is communicate some sort

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:20.970
of very subtle
and specific ideas

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:22.920
that people like Rodney
Brooks have developed

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:23.970
over a number of years.

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:26.740
And the film's like 15 or
more years old at this point.

00:58:26.740 --> 00:58:29.640
So a lot of the
technology is stuff

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:32.760
that is an early
version of stuff

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:35.130
that they're still working
on across the hall there.

00:58:35.130 --> 00:58:37.560
And you'll see much more
advanced versions of it

00:58:37.560 --> 00:58:40.740
in the little videos
that they produce.

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:46.290
But the whole point is that it's
about getting inside his head.

00:58:46.290 --> 00:58:50.160
And yet, it's yet another
frame for documentary film.

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:53.460
And the whole film isn't just
about getting inside his head.

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:54.960
It's about getting
inside this idea.

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:57.340
It's about getting inside
an idea that, again,

00:58:57.340 --> 00:59:00.870
is very, very abstract,
but also very, very

00:59:00.870 --> 00:59:03.390
tactile and tangible.

00:59:03.390 --> 00:59:06.240
How do we, as organisms,
and other organisms,

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:08.730
respond to each other and
respond to the world around us?

00:59:08.730 --> 00:59:10.720
And what does that do?

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:12.790
How does that shape us
and our communities?

00:59:12.790 --> 00:59:15.300
It's again a completely
different way

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:17.250
of doing a documentary film.

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:20.190
Again, I'm presenting it in
kind of an evolutionary way.

00:59:20.190 --> 00:59:23.920
I'm not meaning
to do that at all.

00:59:23.920 --> 00:59:25.830
Even the early stuff--

00:59:25.830 --> 00:59:30.510
I mean, the script-based
filmmaking is alive and well.

00:59:30.510 --> 00:59:32.320
People still do it.

00:59:32.320 --> 00:59:36.850
And it's completely
appropriate for certain things.

00:59:36.850 --> 00:59:39.025
It's just a different
kind of frame.

00:59:39.025 --> 00:59:40.650
It's a different kind
of way of looking

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:46.680
at these ideas and the
choice you make in terms

00:59:46.680 --> 00:59:49.860
of your aesthetics or
your approach are going

00:59:49.860 --> 00:59:53.010
to be determined by who you
are as a filmmaker, who you

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:56.400
develop, who you turn
into as a filmmaker,

00:59:56.400 --> 00:59:59.340
and also what your subject is
and what your interests are.

00:59:59.340 --> 01:00:02.215
If you're Rodney
Brooks and you are--

01:00:02.215 --> 01:00:03.090
or I mean if you're--

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:04.507
I'm sorry-- if
you're Errol Morris

01:00:04.507 --> 01:00:07.500
and you're interested in
these ideas that are, again,

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:10.230
fairly metaphysical,
fairly abstract,

01:00:10.230 --> 01:00:14.790
if you wanted to do that as a
verite film, what would you do?

01:00:14.790 --> 01:00:19.920
Spend months or years sitting
in a lab filming people

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:22.350
doing repetitive tasks.

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:25.140
And you wouldn't really
arrive at the ideas

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:27.060
that you would be
trying to communicate.

01:00:27.060 --> 01:00:29.185
On the other hand, if you're
doing something that's

01:00:29.185 --> 01:00:32.190
very dynamic, like a
presidential campaign,

01:00:32.190 --> 01:00:36.630
it makes a lot of sense
to use a verite technique.

01:00:36.630 --> 01:00:40.890
I want to show one other Errol
Morris clip from a film that

01:00:40.890 --> 01:00:42.090
is much more current.

01:00:42.090 --> 01:00:45.510
It's about five years old,
called the Fog Of War,

01:00:45.510 --> 01:00:49.590
which is essentially a profile
of Robert McNamara, who

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:52.020
is Secretary of Defense
during the Vietnam War.

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:58.980
And as you probably
know, the Vietnam War

01:00:58.980 --> 01:01:04.830
still kind of hangs over our
politics in a lot of ways.

01:01:04.830 --> 01:01:06.540
McNamara is a really
interesting figure

01:01:06.540 --> 01:01:10.230
in that he was front and
center in the war effort

01:01:10.230 --> 01:01:15.900
and made a lot of decisions
that people were extremely upset

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:18.000
with-- many, many people
were extremely upset

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:20.850
with around the world
and in this country.

01:01:20.850 --> 01:01:24.030
People called him a war
criminal for things he did.

01:01:24.030 --> 01:01:27.990
And then late in life, no
more than a few years ago

01:01:27.990 --> 01:01:31.770
when he was in his early
80s, he kind of came

01:01:31.770 --> 01:01:34.320
to a different understanding
about what he had done

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:42.630
and didn't quite kind of
accept blame or present himself

01:01:42.630 --> 01:01:45.430
as someone who was guilty
of doing certain things,

01:01:45.430 --> 01:01:49.927
but definitely ended up with a
much more complex understanding

01:01:49.927 --> 01:01:50.760
of what he had done.

01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:52.890
And the film is a
fascinating film.

01:01:52.890 --> 01:01:54.580
He's the only person in it.

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:56.550
He's the only interview in it.

01:01:56.550 --> 01:02:01.422
And it's really about a person,
a man, struggling with his past

01:02:01.422 --> 01:02:02.880
and struggling to
make sense of it,

01:02:02.880 --> 01:02:05.520
without fully accepting
maybe some of the things

01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:06.881
that he may have done.

01:02:06.881 --> 01:02:08.839
But I'm just going to
show a little clip of it.

01:02:08.839 --> 01:02:11.653
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:17.330
- While I was secretary,
we used what's

01:02:17.330 --> 01:02:21.910
called Agent Orange in
Vietnam, the chemical

01:02:21.910 --> 01:02:23.650
that strips leaves off of trees.

01:02:26.690 --> 01:02:31.400
After the war it is claimed
that was a toxic chemical.

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:36.590
And it killed many individuals,
soldiers and civilians

01:02:36.590 --> 01:02:40.000
exposed to it.

01:02:40.000 --> 01:02:43.330
Were those who
issued the approval

01:02:43.330 --> 01:02:46.420
to use Agent Orange criminals?

01:02:46.420 --> 01:02:48.645
Were they committing a
crime against humanity?

01:02:51.560 --> 01:02:53.680
Let's look at the law.

01:02:53.680 --> 01:02:55.870
Now what kind of
a law do we have

01:02:55.870 --> 01:02:58.660
that says these chemicals
are acceptable for use in war

01:02:58.660 --> 01:03:00.730
and these chemicals are not?

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:03.200
We don't have clear
definitions of that kind.

01:03:03.200 --> 01:03:10.120
I never in the world would have
authorized an illegal action.

01:03:10.120 --> 01:03:12.100
I'm not really sure I
authorized Agent Orange.

01:03:12.100 --> 01:03:13.090
I don't remember it.

01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:14.950
But it certainly occurred--

01:03:14.950 --> 01:03:16.990
the use of it occurred
while I was secretary.

01:03:16.990 --> 01:03:19.325
[END PLAYBACK]

01:03:21.660 --> 01:03:23.820
CHRIS BOEBEL: This
is Errol Morris,

01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:26.430
he's really interested
in an internal state,

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:29.290
a man struggling with himself,
struggling with his past.

01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:33.310
It's a film set in a person's
mind, in a lot of ways.

01:03:33.310 --> 01:03:36.210
Even though it's about Vietnam,
it's about a very, very real

01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:39.030
and horrifying event.

01:03:39.030 --> 01:03:42.030
It's really in a lot of
ways set in McNamara's mind.

01:03:42.030 --> 01:03:47.010
So his approach is
really, really interwoven

01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:51.000
with what he's interested
in as a filmmaker, which

01:03:51.000 --> 01:03:53.370
is interwoven with his
technique, what he puts

01:03:53.370 --> 01:03:54.960
in the frame quote unquote.

01:03:54.960 --> 01:03:59.790
And again, the last point
that I'll make with these

01:03:59.790 --> 01:04:02.280
is that it's obvious-- it
should be apparent by now

01:04:02.280 --> 01:04:06.150
that filmmakers are not
just in conversation

01:04:06.150 --> 01:04:08.970
or they don't just
build a relationship

01:04:08.970 --> 01:04:10.050
with their subjects.

01:04:10.050 --> 01:04:13.470
They're also in conversation
with each other.

01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:17.510
So Errol Morris is very
much defining himself as not

01:04:17.510 --> 01:04:19.610
the cinema verite person.

01:04:19.610 --> 01:04:22.310
And Robert Drew and
the other people

01:04:22.310 --> 01:04:25.880
who pioneered verite in
the '60s were very much

01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:28.850
defining themselves as not
the TV documentary producers.

01:04:28.850 --> 01:04:32.060
We don't do talking
head documentaries.

01:04:32.060 --> 01:04:37.610
And someone like Edward R.
Murrow was presenting himself,

01:04:37.610 --> 01:04:40.472
and in fact he was, in print
as well, as a journalist,

01:04:40.472 --> 01:04:42.680
as someone who actually went
out and got stories that

01:04:42.680 --> 01:04:44.960
were dramatic and
human and real, as

01:04:44.960 --> 01:04:47.083
opposed to these kind of
newsreel presentations

01:04:47.083 --> 01:04:49.250
where everything was just
kind of laid on a platter.

01:04:49.250 --> 01:04:52.880
So people define themselves in
opposition to what they like.

01:04:52.880 --> 01:04:55.490
They emulate what
they like they like.

01:04:55.490 --> 01:04:56.990
They define themselves
in opposition

01:04:56.990 --> 01:04:59.280
to what they don't like.

01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:02.180
And that's a conversation
that just goes on.

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:06.440
I want to show just a couple
of other quick clips, for which

01:05:06.440 --> 01:05:12.850
I'm going to go to YouTube,
because I think it's worth

01:05:12.850 --> 01:05:17.470
also talking about how
that conversation also

01:05:17.470 --> 01:05:19.570
crosses genres.

01:05:19.570 --> 01:05:22.210
It doesn't happen just
within documentary film

01:05:22.210 --> 01:05:23.470
or commercial film.

01:05:23.470 --> 01:05:28.960
But people obviously
are continually

01:05:28.960 --> 01:05:31.030
affected by each other.

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:36.660
Has anybody seen
this next film I'm

01:05:36.660 --> 01:05:39.510
going to show, Powers Of 10?

01:05:39.510 --> 01:05:42.900
It's a classic
science documentary.

01:05:42.900 --> 01:05:44.850
And it also has
an MIT connection.

01:05:44.850 --> 01:05:47.430
It's narrated by
Philip Morrison.

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:50.370
I think it was made in 1977.

01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:54.720
And he was a legendary
physics prof at MIT.

01:05:54.720 --> 01:05:57.900
And it's a film
that is basically

01:05:57.900 --> 01:06:03.943
designed to give people a
sense of, well, powers of 10.

01:06:03.943 --> 01:06:04.860
But I'll just show it.

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:06.073
It's short and pretty cool.

01:06:06.073 --> 01:06:07.740
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
isn't this the one

01:06:07.740 --> 01:06:09.810
that they used to have all
MIT undergraduates watch

01:06:09.810 --> 01:06:10.560
when they entered?

01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:13.410
CHRIS BOEBEL: They did
up until like, maybe

01:06:13.410 --> 01:06:17.190
even the early '90s, all
MIT incoming freshmen

01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:22.113
on their first or second
day had to watch this.

01:06:22.113 --> 01:06:24.030
I think they all went
into Kresge and sat down

01:06:24.030 --> 01:06:26.080
and watched this film.

01:06:26.080 --> 01:06:26.830
And it's worth it.

01:06:26.830 --> 01:06:27.990
It's very good.

01:06:27.990 --> 01:06:31.140
I mean, keep in mind, that in
terms of the special effects,

01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:35.460
it's made it's made
close to 40 years ago.

01:06:35.460 --> 01:06:37.350
But it's a very,
very cool concept.

01:06:45.125 --> 01:06:46.500
It's a really
interesting example

01:06:46.500 --> 01:06:50.760
of illustrating visually
a fairly abstract

01:06:50.760 --> 01:06:53.160
mathematical concept.

01:06:53.160 --> 01:06:55.950
And I wanted to just quickly
show another clip that I

01:06:55.950 --> 01:07:01.380
think is a fun takeoff on this.

01:07:01.380 --> 01:07:03.990
Does anybody remember, or did
anybody see the Jodie Foster

01:07:03.990 --> 01:07:05.850
movie Contact?

01:07:05.850 --> 01:07:07.920
It's a science fiction
film in which Jodie Foster

01:07:07.920 --> 01:07:08.850
gets to meet aliens.

01:07:08.850 --> 01:07:10.890
I won't say any more than that.

01:07:10.890 --> 01:07:13.290
This is the opening of
the film and the idea

01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:21.600
is to give a general
audience a sense of how

01:07:21.600 --> 01:07:23.820
radio waves or
television signals

01:07:23.820 --> 01:07:26.130
travel away from the Earth
at the speed of light.

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:28.410
And aside from being
wildly scientifically

01:07:28.410 --> 01:07:31.590
inaccurate, which
is kind of funny,

01:07:31.590 --> 01:07:35.670
the film is-- it's just an
interesting kind of Hollywood

01:07:35.670 --> 01:07:37.530
take off on powers of
10, I think clearly

01:07:37.530 --> 01:07:39.720
inspired by Powers of 10.

01:07:45.960 --> 01:07:49.600
So I didn't realize that
Saturn was 50 light years away

01:07:49.600 --> 01:07:50.100
from Earth.

01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:54.640
But I think that it's cool
for a couple of reasons.

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:56.910
One, it's obviously
inspired by Powers Of 10.

01:07:56.910 --> 01:08:00.060
But also it's
really kind of gutsy

01:08:00.060 --> 01:08:02.610
because I think it's one
of the only mainstream

01:08:02.610 --> 01:08:04.087
commercial Hollywood
movies that I

01:08:04.087 --> 01:08:06.170
know of that has actually
like a minute and a half

01:08:06.170 --> 01:08:09.120
of absolute silence,
like nothing

01:08:09.120 --> 01:08:12.095
on the soundtrack at
all, which is cool.

01:08:12.095 --> 01:08:13.470
And I'm sure they
could care less

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:17.160
that they got the speed
of light completely wrong

01:08:17.160 --> 01:08:24.180
and that it's very variable
and that the Volare from 1953

01:08:24.180 --> 01:08:26.010
is traveling by Saturn.

01:08:26.010 --> 01:08:30.660
But anyway, the main thing
I just wanted to point out

01:08:30.660 --> 01:08:34.590
was that these ideas
travel and that's

01:08:34.590 --> 01:08:38.310
something that you can take
advantage of in your own work.

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:42.237
You can steal from
whomever you like,

01:08:42.237 --> 01:08:44.279
and certainly be influenced
by whomever you like.

01:08:44.279 --> 01:08:46.529
We're going to be
looking at a lot of films

01:08:46.529 --> 01:08:51.270
in this class, from classic
documentaries, from the 1920s

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:54.540
and '30s up through
stuff that was

01:08:54.540 --> 01:08:59.979
posted on YouTube or TechTV just
a few weeks ago or whatever.

01:08:59.979 --> 01:09:02.970
And all of this is stuff--
it's grist for the mill for you

01:09:02.970 --> 01:09:04.109
to be influenced by.

01:09:04.109 --> 01:09:07.649
And one of the whole points
of, again, looking at clips

01:09:07.649 --> 01:09:09.300
and talking about
them and trying

01:09:09.300 --> 01:09:15.300
to engage with the broad trends
and ideas in documentary film

01:09:15.300 --> 01:09:16.890
on Wednesday night
is so that you

01:09:16.890 --> 01:09:19.620
can come in on Friday
morning in the lab

01:09:19.620 --> 01:09:24.060
and have that informing you as
you start playing with cameras,

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:26.880
learning about editing,
learning about sound recording.

01:09:29.430 --> 01:09:31.290
Because that's
what makes it fun.

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:33.779
The technical side can
be very frustrating.

01:09:33.779 --> 01:09:37.270
As I said, there is a
steep learning curve,

01:09:37.270 --> 01:09:40.800
which we'll try to help you get
over as smoothly as possible.

01:09:40.800 --> 01:09:42.689
But what's really,
really exciting

01:09:42.689 --> 01:09:46.830
and fun is just being aware of
all the possibilities and all

01:09:46.830 --> 01:09:50.250
of the power that you have
in the choices that you

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:52.779
make as a filmmaker.

01:09:52.779 --> 01:09:57.690
So want to continue, then?

01:09:57.690 --> 01:09:59.880
Or shall we take
another little break?

01:09:59.880 --> 01:10:01.297
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So that was kind

01:10:01.297 --> 01:10:04.080
of our overview on two ends.

01:10:04.080 --> 01:10:09.840
Usually mostly when we have
our classes on Wednesday night,

01:10:09.840 --> 01:10:11.500
it's basically going
to be discussion.

01:10:11.500 --> 01:10:13.950
So this is more
lecturing than we're ever

01:10:13.950 --> 01:10:17.690
going to do any point
other than the semester.

01:10:17.690 --> 01:10:19.800
But just to get a bunch
of ideas and to get

01:10:19.800 --> 01:10:21.750
you thinking about
things, that's

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:24.190
how we wanted to begin things.

01:10:24.190 --> 01:10:25.800
So now we're just
going to jump right

01:10:25.800 --> 01:10:29.190
in and we're going to start
out with looking at both some

01:10:29.190 --> 01:10:35.760
very early film clips
that people would now

01:10:35.760 --> 01:10:38.282
refer to as documentary,
although at the time

01:10:38.282 --> 01:10:39.990
they weren't conceived
of as documentary.

01:10:39.990 --> 01:10:44.760
The word documentary wasn't
really coined into the 1930s.

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:48.720
But we're going to be looking
at some early, very early,

01:10:48.720 --> 01:10:52.080
some of the very first
film clips from the 1890s,

01:10:52.080 --> 01:10:53.460
and then a little
bit after that,

01:10:53.460 --> 01:10:56.730
some of the very first films
that were made about science,

01:10:56.730 --> 01:10:59.160
documentary films, again
that we would refer

01:10:59.160 --> 01:11:02.530
to as documentary films now.

01:11:02.530 --> 01:11:04.030
Should I just hop in.

01:11:04.030 --> 01:11:04.840
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

01:11:04.840 --> 01:11:05.980
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

01:11:05.980 --> 01:11:09.440
OK, so we're just going to spend
a few minutes on the Lumiere

01:11:09.440 --> 01:11:09.940
brothers.

01:11:09.940 --> 01:11:12.970
I don't if any of you
have ever heard of them.

01:11:12.970 --> 01:11:15.910
But you might already know
that film cameras were largely

01:11:15.910 --> 01:11:17.650
invented in the 1890s.

01:11:17.650 --> 01:11:19.540
But they were inspired
by older technologies.

01:11:19.540 --> 01:11:23.320
So some of you might be familiar
with magic lanterns or lanterns

01:11:23.320 --> 01:11:26.260
that had a candle inside.

01:11:26.260 --> 01:11:29.430
But they might have
cutouts around it

01:11:29.430 --> 01:11:31.430
in a circle that would
be projected onto a wall,

01:11:31.430 --> 01:11:32.050
for example.

01:11:32.050 --> 01:11:33.220
Or you had like flip books.

01:11:33.220 --> 01:11:34.750
You know what flip books were?

01:11:34.750 --> 01:11:37.480
Where you would have different
pictures, might be a horse

01:11:37.480 --> 01:11:40.865
galloping, a horse in
different stages of running.

01:11:40.865 --> 01:11:42.490
And then you'd have
different pictures.

01:11:42.490 --> 01:11:43.990
And then you'd flip
them really fast

01:11:43.990 --> 01:11:45.790
and would look like
the horse was moving.

01:11:45.790 --> 01:11:47.832
So there were these earlier
kinds of technologies

01:11:47.832 --> 01:11:49.360
that preceded film cameras.

01:11:49.360 --> 01:11:52.930
But film cameras as a
technology were largely

01:11:52.930 --> 01:11:53.860
invented in the 1890s.

01:11:53.860 --> 01:11:55.735
There were a number of
inventors who were all

01:11:55.735 --> 01:11:58.420
working simultaneously, trying
to beat each other to get

01:11:58.420 --> 01:11:59.605
the film cameras out there.

01:12:02.170 --> 01:12:05.590
But two of the
most famous people

01:12:05.590 --> 01:12:07.485
that you're going to
hear about in relation

01:12:07.485 --> 01:12:08.860
to this are the
Lumiere brothers,

01:12:08.860 --> 01:12:12.430
and in particular Louis
Lumiere, who invented a camera

01:12:12.430 --> 01:12:14.152
called the cinematograph.

01:12:14.152 --> 01:12:16.360
And that's actually where
the word cinema comes from,

01:12:16.360 --> 01:12:17.050
cinematograph.

01:12:17.050 --> 01:12:18.175
That's where it comes from.

01:12:18.175 --> 01:12:19.010
They were French.

01:12:19.010 --> 01:12:22.067
This was in 1895, 1894, '95.

01:12:22.067 --> 01:12:23.650
And one of the things
that we're going

01:12:23.650 --> 01:12:25.192
to be discussing
throughout the class

01:12:25.192 --> 01:12:27.730
is how particular technologies
get created because people

01:12:27.730 --> 01:12:30.287
have the desire to do certain
things with those technologies.

01:12:30.287 --> 01:12:31.870
But in turn, the
technologies that get

01:12:31.870 --> 01:12:34.150
created then also
influence then what

01:12:34.150 --> 01:12:36.040
people are able to do
with those technologies

01:12:36.040 --> 01:12:37.960
and how they end
up getting used.

01:12:37.960 --> 01:12:39.910
And so that's something
to be thinking about

01:12:39.910 --> 01:12:42.820
with this kind of camera
and another camera

01:12:42.820 --> 01:12:44.470
that we're going to see shortly.

01:12:44.470 --> 01:12:48.100
The technology, the
invention itself strongly

01:12:48.100 --> 01:12:51.070
shaped how the camera
was going to get used.

01:12:51.070 --> 01:12:54.370
And we can show you an example
of this what the Lumiere.

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:58.930
This is some images
of the cinematograph.

01:12:58.930 --> 01:13:01.325
OK, and what's
distinctive about it?

01:13:01.325 --> 01:13:03.700
I mean anything you notice
just on looking at the camera?

01:13:08.810 --> 01:13:11.310
And you're actually going to
be reading a very short chapter

01:13:11.310 --> 01:13:13.170
about this for next week, which
will give you a little bit

01:13:13.170 --> 01:13:14.337
more background information.

01:13:14.337 --> 01:13:18.350
But one thing was that it was
made it was made out of wood.

01:13:18.350 --> 01:13:19.350
It was relatively light.

01:13:19.350 --> 01:13:22.320
It was about 16 pounds.

01:13:22.320 --> 01:13:23.940
And so it was portable.

01:13:23.940 --> 01:13:27.260
You could carry it around.

01:13:27.260 --> 01:13:31.130
In addition to that, so you see
the guy with the camera there,

01:13:31.130 --> 01:13:32.510
no electricity, right?

01:13:32.510 --> 01:13:34.010
No cord.

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:35.870
It was hand-cranked.

01:13:35.870 --> 01:13:37.800
So you didn't need
electricity to do it.

01:13:37.800 --> 01:13:42.213
And also, something interesting
about this technology

01:13:42.213 --> 01:13:43.630
was in addition
to being a camera,

01:13:43.630 --> 01:13:45.950
it was also could be
converted into a projector

01:13:45.950 --> 01:13:49.160
at the same time, and
also a film processor.

01:13:49.160 --> 01:13:52.058
So you could-- use so you could
take images on it one day.

01:13:52.058 --> 01:13:54.350
And the next day you could
project them to the audience

01:13:54.350 --> 01:13:56.870
and show them images
of themselves.

01:13:56.870 --> 01:13:58.490
And because it
was mobile, it was

01:13:58.490 --> 01:14:01.452
possible to take it out in
the world and take it outside.

01:14:01.452 --> 01:14:03.410
And actually was even
better to take it outside

01:14:03.410 --> 01:14:08.108
because you needed light to
be able to take good images.

01:14:08.108 --> 01:14:09.650
So it was actually
better if you were

01:14:09.650 --> 01:14:13.050
outside using this invention.

01:14:13.050 --> 01:14:16.850
And so because of this, many of
the very first film clips that

01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:20.120
were taken with
this invention were

01:14:20.120 --> 01:14:22.870
what were called actualities,
that film scholars often

01:14:22.870 --> 01:14:23.870
refer to as actualities.

01:14:23.870 --> 01:14:25.340
But you might think of
them as the precursors

01:14:25.340 --> 01:14:26.270
to documentary film.

01:14:26.270 --> 01:14:29.630
So they're little snippets
of daily life, sort of life

01:14:29.630 --> 01:14:30.830
out on the fly, outside.

01:14:30.830 --> 01:14:33.560
And again, the
camera's moving around.

01:14:33.560 --> 01:14:35.540
The reels were really
small, so largely they

01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:38.420
were one minute in the
1890s when these first film

01:14:38.420 --> 01:14:40.670
clips came out.

01:14:40.670 --> 01:14:41.843
And something else--

01:14:41.843 --> 01:14:43.760
I don't think we have--
that's the only images

01:14:43.760 --> 01:14:45.830
that we have the camera, right?

01:14:45.830 --> 01:14:47.960
Something else that's
interesting to think about--

01:14:47.960 --> 01:14:50.900
because there was a lot
of competition at the time

01:14:50.900 --> 01:14:57.470
to invent the moving picture
camera, the Lumiere brothers,

01:14:57.470 --> 01:15:00.920
their family had
had a photography--

01:15:00.920 --> 01:15:02.570
and they made
photographic equipment.

01:15:02.570 --> 01:15:04.010
So they had a factory in France.

01:15:04.010 --> 01:15:05.120
They were business people.

01:15:05.120 --> 01:15:06.870
They knew this was
going to be a big deal.

01:15:06.870 --> 01:15:08.390
People were really
excited about it.

01:15:08.390 --> 01:15:12.380
So they were very
keen business people

01:15:12.380 --> 01:15:14.520
in terms of what they
did with the camera.

01:15:14.520 --> 01:15:16.010
And so they started
doing showings

01:15:16.010 --> 01:15:18.050
in Paris of some
of the very early,

01:15:18.050 --> 01:15:22.010
the very first little
one-minute films that they shot.

01:15:22.010 --> 01:15:23.750
And they did these
screenings in Paris

01:15:23.750 --> 01:15:25.970
that caused a lot of interest.

01:15:25.970 --> 01:15:29.240
And then they trained, I
forget exactly how many,

01:15:29.240 --> 01:15:32.653
40, 50 people early on,
to use the cinematograph.

01:15:32.653 --> 01:15:34.070
And then they sent
them out-- they

01:15:34.070 --> 01:15:36.770
made enough of
these inventions--

01:15:36.770 --> 01:15:39.630
and then they sent them
out all over the world.

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:43.188
So from the very beginning,
it was quite international.

01:15:43.188 --> 01:15:45.230
I mean certainly all over
Europe, and in addition

01:15:45.230 --> 01:15:46.880
to that, relatively
shortly thereafter,

01:15:46.880 --> 01:15:50.940
they sent some of their
operators to Tunisia, to Egypt.

01:15:50.940 --> 01:15:54.500
I'm trying to remember where
else they went, a number of--

01:15:54.500 --> 01:15:57.330
India, Bombay from
very early on.

01:15:57.330 --> 01:15:59.720
And so these operators,
they would go out

01:15:59.720 --> 01:16:01.530
and they would film
what was going on

01:16:01.530 --> 01:16:02.780
say in Bombay and the capital.

01:16:02.780 --> 01:16:03.988
And then they would have--

01:16:03.988 --> 01:16:05.780
they would rent a
theater for the next day.

01:16:05.780 --> 01:16:07.070
And then they would
show to the audiences.

01:16:07.070 --> 01:16:09.695
And they would advertise them to
the audiences come to yourself

01:16:09.695 --> 01:16:10.360
on film.

01:16:10.360 --> 01:16:12.350
And so you had these
huge lines, everybody

01:16:12.350 --> 01:16:13.600
wanted to go see these things.

01:16:13.600 --> 01:16:15.620
They created a huge buzz
all around the world.

01:16:15.620 --> 01:16:19.273
And then after they
created a huge buzz--

01:16:19.273 --> 01:16:20.690
until that point
they, had refused

01:16:20.690 --> 01:16:23.065
to sell any of these cameras.

01:16:23.065 --> 01:16:24.440
And then after
that, they started

01:16:24.440 --> 01:16:25.850
manufacturing the
cameras and selling them

01:16:25.850 --> 01:16:27.267
once they had
created a huge buzz.

01:16:27.267 --> 01:16:30.230
So it was very keen business
strategy on their part,

01:16:30.230 --> 01:16:33.287
how they did this.

01:16:33.287 --> 01:16:35.870
CHRIS BOEBEL: You can think of
it as kind of an early YouTube.

01:16:35.870 --> 01:16:37.640
I mean, it was.

01:16:37.640 --> 01:16:40.610
Had a lot of the same
function as YouTube

01:16:40.610 --> 01:16:41.840
and a lot of the same appeal.

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:44.420
You could see yourself on film.

01:16:44.420 --> 01:16:47.810
And it also opened up
an ability to see others

01:16:47.810 --> 01:16:50.660
and to see people living
on other continents

01:16:50.660 --> 01:16:53.790
and other countries, people
from other cultures very,

01:16:53.790 --> 01:16:54.590
very easily.

01:16:54.590 --> 01:16:56.660
And it seemed almost
magical at the time.

01:16:56.660 --> 01:16:58.490
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, and
that international focus,

01:16:58.490 --> 01:16:59.840
one thing to keep in
mind at the time this

01:16:59.840 --> 01:17:01.430
was the 1890s was
also the height

01:17:01.430 --> 01:17:03.710
of the European colonial period.

01:17:03.710 --> 01:17:08.660
So also pretty quickly
documentary films

01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:10.670
ended up become--

01:17:10.670 --> 01:17:13.700
they were shown in the colonies
but they were also often showed

01:17:13.700 --> 01:17:17.420
a certain vision of images of
the colonial powers in relation

01:17:17.420 --> 01:17:19.950
to the natives, quote unquote,
who were in the colony.

01:17:19.950 --> 01:17:21.710
So you have to keep in mind
it was very international.

01:17:21.710 --> 01:17:23.460
But this was also the
international period

01:17:23.460 --> 01:17:25.928
at that point, was a colonial
world where you had--

01:17:25.928 --> 01:17:28.220
Europe was very dominant over
other parts of the world.

01:17:32.477 --> 01:17:35.060
But in addition to that, I mean
there was a very strong impact

01:17:35.060 --> 01:17:35.730
of these images.

01:17:35.730 --> 01:17:37.100
So for example, if you
think about Bombay,

01:17:37.100 --> 01:17:39.058
I don't know how many of
you watch Hindi films.

01:17:39.058 --> 01:17:42.077
Are you interested in
watching Bollywood films?

01:17:42.077 --> 01:17:43.160
Any of you have seen them?

01:17:45.860 --> 01:17:48.717
So the Bombay film industry is
actually bigger than Hollywood.

01:17:48.717 --> 01:17:51.050
And actually, at this point,
I believe the Nigerian film

01:17:51.050 --> 01:17:53.450
industry has also surpassed
Hollywood in terms

01:17:53.450 --> 01:17:56.160
of number of films per year.

01:17:56.160 --> 01:17:58.220
Those are sort of more
films made on the fly.

01:17:58.220 --> 01:18:02.580
But the Bombay film industry
is absolutely enormous.

01:18:02.580 --> 01:18:05.060
And it has a very,
very long history

01:18:05.060 --> 01:18:10.910
of filmmaking that goes back to
the early Lumiere operators who

01:18:10.910 --> 01:18:12.500
went there with
the cinematograph

01:18:12.500 --> 01:18:13.542
and we're showing things.

01:18:13.542 --> 01:18:16.350
And then they developed their
own internal film industry

01:18:16.350 --> 01:18:18.800
you coming out of that lineage.

01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:21.530
But anyway, let's just
show a couple early, very

01:18:21.530 --> 01:18:26.320
earliest clips, maybe some
sense of what got filmed.

01:18:26.320 --> 01:18:30.040
So this is the Lumiere
photographic equipment factory,

01:18:30.040 --> 01:18:33.290
so the workers leaving that.

01:18:33.290 --> 01:18:35.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh, yeah.

01:18:35.020 --> 01:18:36.937
CHRISTINE WALLEY: You
want to pause that for--

01:19:31.620 --> 01:19:34.130
CHRIS BOEBEL: So this is
one of the very early films.

01:19:34.130 --> 01:19:36.890
And you may have heard
this apocryphal stories is

01:19:36.890 --> 01:19:39.900
about audiences ducking
when the train goes by.

01:19:39.900 --> 01:19:43.100
This is the actual
film that supposedly

01:19:43.100 --> 01:19:47.480
had had audiences ducking to
get out of the way of the train

01:19:47.480 --> 01:19:49.845
as it pulled in.

01:19:49.845 --> 01:19:51.720
One of the things that
I think is really cool

01:19:51.720 --> 01:19:54.905
about these films is that people
aren't used to being filmed.

01:19:54.905 --> 01:19:56.530
They don't really
know what's going on.

01:19:56.530 --> 01:19:58.113
So they're completely
unselfconscious.

01:19:58.113 --> 01:20:01.460
They're not mugging they're
not awkward for the most part.

01:20:01.460 --> 01:20:02.460
They're not ill at ease.

01:20:02.460 --> 01:20:03.960
They're just doing what they do.

01:20:03.960 --> 01:20:05.970
And they aren't really sure
what the guy with the wooden box

01:20:05.970 --> 01:20:06.470
is doing.

01:21:36.855 --> 01:21:39.230
CHRISTINE WALLEY: We're going
to pause for just a second.

01:21:39.230 --> 01:21:40.520
I was just going
to let you watch

01:21:40.520 --> 01:21:41.990
and then you could
comment on it later.

01:21:41.990 --> 01:21:43.580
But that last one,
just to mention,

01:21:43.580 --> 01:21:46.010
some people refer to that as
actually the first narrative

01:21:46.010 --> 01:21:48.350
film in the sense that they
had kind of figured out

01:21:48.350 --> 01:21:52.220
a little story plot that they
wanted to act out beforehand.

01:21:52.220 --> 01:21:57.140
But there's one other one
I want to see, maybe--

01:21:57.140 --> 01:21:58.520
Demolition of a Wall, OK.

01:22:33.080 --> 01:22:35.300
OK, and what's interesting
about that one,

01:22:35.300 --> 01:22:37.370
because the cameras
were cranked you,

01:22:37.370 --> 01:22:40.640
can also run them in reverse,
or you could speed them up.

01:22:40.640 --> 01:22:42.560
So this is something
that the operators would

01:22:42.560 --> 01:22:44.492
do to amuse the audiences.

01:22:44.492 --> 01:22:45.950
So with the demolition
of the wall,

01:22:45.950 --> 01:22:46.790
they would have
the wall come down.

01:22:46.790 --> 01:22:48.650
And then they would reverse and
have the wall go back up, which

01:22:48.650 --> 01:22:49.790
was like a really big hit.

01:22:49.790 --> 01:22:51.360
I thought that was really funny.

01:22:51.360 --> 01:22:54.050
So you could speed things
up to make things funny.

01:22:54.050 --> 01:22:55.700
We didn't show any
international ones.

01:22:55.700 --> 01:22:56.780
We should show one.

01:22:56.780 --> 01:22:59.180
I always get [INAUDIBLE].

01:22:59.180 --> 01:23:00.850
I mean I don't think
I've seen that one.

01:23:03.630 --> 01:23:06.730
New York, just for fun.

01:23:06.730 --> 01:23:08.353
CHRIS BOEBEL: Panorama.

01:23:08.353 --> 01:23:10.520
CHRIS BOEBEL: There's Union
Square, for those of you

01:23:10.520 --> 01:23:11.812
who are familiar with New York.

01:23:17.160 --> 01:23:18.930
Anyway, but there's
a number of them

01:23:18.930 --> 01:23:25.470
also where you see royalty,
you see military troops

01:23:25.470 --> 01:23:26.490
on maneuver.

01:23:26.490 --> 01:23:29.420
You see just little
snippets of life.

01:23:29.420 --> 01:23:33.013
And so these are some of
the very early actualities

01:23:33.013 --> 01:23:34.680
that were made by the
Lumiere operators.

01:23:34.680 --> 01:23:36.870
So just general
reactions comments

01:23:36.870 --> 01:23:38.957
on things you thought
they were interesting?

01:23:48.843 --> 01:23:49.760
Are they fun to watch?

01:23:49.760 --> 01:23:55.310
Are they-- I mean
why are they fun?

01:23:55.310 --> 01:23:58.058
Or anything surprising?

01:23:58.058 --> 01:24:00.280
AUDIENCE: Home video--

01:24:00.280 --> 01:24:02.950
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, what
makes that home video-like?

01:24:02.950 --> 01:24:06.015
AUDIENCE: It's kind of
little moments that are--

01:24:06.015 --> 01:24:07.390
like if you were
related to them,

01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:11.010
it being more valuable
to you [INAUDIBLE]..

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:12.510
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah and there's--

01:24:12.510 --> 01:24:14.010
actually, we didn't see
one, but there's one with--

01:24:14.010 --> 01:24:15.000
I don't think it's
on this disk--

01:24:15.000 --> 01:24:16.590
but there's one
of a sack raised.

01:24:16.590 --> 01:24:20.280
I'm not sure if it was the
workers at the Lumiere factory,

01:24:20.280 --> 01:24:21.090
who exactly it was.

01:24:21.090 --> 01:24:26.280
But there's all these
guys in the sack race and

01:24:26.280 --> 01:24:27.540
falling down and laughing.

01:24:27.540 --> 01:24:28.690
And they're laughing
at the camera.

01:24:28.690 --> 01:24:30.732
And they're totally mugging
it up for the camera.

01:24:30.732 --> 01:24:33.910
And that definitely has
a home video quality.

01:24:33.910 --> 01:24:36.457
So why does that have
a home video quality?

01:24:36.457 --> 01:24:38.040
If you think back
to when we were just

01:24:38.040 --> 01:24:40.123
talking about the David
MacDougall, the responsive

01:24:40.123 --> 01:24:44.940
camera, the interactive camera,
it's an interactive camera.

01:24:44.940 --> 01:24:49.320
Particularly early on,
with leaving the factory,

01:24:49.320 --> 01:24:51.570
some people are looking and
then other people are not,

01:24:51.570 --> 01:24:53.987
so you don't know how many
people are really aware of what

01:24:53.987 --> 01:24:55.805
the contraption does yet.

01:24:55.805 --> 01:24:57.180
But a little bit
later on, people

01:24:57.180 --> 01:24:59.195
are very aware of what
this thing is doing,

01:24:59.195 --> 01:25:01.320
and they really start
hamming it up for the camera.

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:03.153
So it's the sense of
this interactive camera

01:25:03.153 --> 01:25:06.620
where people are not pretending
that the camera's not there.

01:25:06.620 --> 01:25:08.180
They're interacting
with the camera.

01:25:08.180 --> 01:25:09.180
And they think it's fun.

01:25:09.180 --> 01:25:11.370
And this is that's something
that really switches later

01:25:11.370 --> 01:25:13.745
with both fiction films and
documentary films, where they

01:25:13.745 --> 01:25:17.342
both of them increasingly become
about creating interior worlds

01:25:17.342 --> 01:25:19.050
where the focus in a
lot of fiction films

01:25:19.050 --> 01:25:21.120
is about creating the
sense of an interior world,

01:25:21.120 --> 01:25:22.560
where you're getting in some--

01:25:22.560 --> 01:25:24.090
inside some other world.

01:25:24.090 --> 01:25:26.423
And you don't want to
really have attention

01:25:26.423 --> 01:25:27.840
being called to
the fact that this

01:25:27.840 --> 01:25:29.280
is being filmed necessarily.

01:25:29.280 --> 01:25:31.620
And a lot of
documentary film follows

01:25:31.620 --> 01:25:33.900
that those ideas as well.

01:25:33.900 --> 01:25:35.760
But early on, again,
there's no distinction

01:25:35.760 --> 01:25:38.190
here between fiction film
and documentary film,

01:25:38.190 --> 01:25:39.840
because this was just invented.

01:25:39.840 --> 01:25:44.640
So the little bit with
the hose, acting that out,

01:25:44.640 --> 01:25:46.692
I mean there's not
a sense whether it's

01:25:46.692 --> 01:25:47.650
fiction or documentary.

01:25:47.650 --> 01:25:48.640
Say, hey, let's act this out.

01:25:48.640 --> 01:25:49.320
This would be really fun.

01:25:49.320 --> 01:25:51.540
And presumably the feeding
the baby gets talked about

01:25:51.540 --> 01:25:55.205
is another one that was,
talked about it beforehand.

01:25:55.205 --> 01:25:56.580
This would be a
good thing to do.

01:25:56.580 --> 01:25:57.570
Let's go feed the baby.

01:25:57.570 --> 01:26:00.360
I think that's one
of the Lumiere--

01:26:00.360 --> 01:26:01.860
I forget if it's
one of the brothers

01:26:01.860 --> 01:26:03.193
or one of their other relatives.

01:26:03.193 --> 01:26:08.033
But no strict separation at
this point between fiction

01:26:08.033 --> 01:26:08.700
and documentary.

01:26:08.700 --> 01:26:11.280
That distinction
doesn't really exist,

01:26:11.280 --> 01:26:14.520
or presumptions about how
people should be interacting

01:26:14.520 --> 01:26:16.770
with the camera necessarily.

01:26:16.770 --> 01:26:19.110
Anything else that you
guys noticed or thought

01:26:19.110 --> 01:26:20.610
was interesting?

01:26:20.610 --> 01:26:22.680
AUDIENCE: Just to build
up-- what was her name?

01:26:22.680 --> 01:26:23.370
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Sabrina.

01:26:23.370 --> 01:26:24.787
AUDIENCE: Well,
Sabrina said, it's

01:26:24.787 --> 01:26:26.700
interesting that
most of the scenes

01:26:26.700 --> 01:26:29.460
are very kind of
daily life, mundane.

01:26:29.460 --> 01:26:33.210
So like you have a camera, the
first thing you want to film

01:26:33.210 --> 01:26:36.660
is yourself, your city,
your family, kind of funny

01:26:36.660 --> 01:26:39.540
and joke in the garden rather
than feeling like grand things.

01:26:39.540 --> 01:26:41.612
And I thought it was very nice.

01:26:41.612 --> 01:26:43.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
what's interesting is

01:26:43.320 --> 01:26:47.400
that as the Lumiere
business progressed,

01:26:47.400 --> 01:26:50.100
they started doing more
and more grand things,

01:26:50.100 --> 01:26:55.590
like coronations and affairs
of state and visits by royalty.

01:26:55.590 --> 01:26:57.497
So it became more news-reely.

01:26:57.497 --> 01:26:59.830
So the early stuff is exactly
what you're talking about.

01:26:59.830 --> 01:27:03.133
And then it kind of evolved into
something a little different.

01:27:03.133 --> 01:27:05.550
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, and
sometimes it gets talked about

01:27:05.550 --> 01:27:09.827
is that royalty and the
wealthy came to see,

01:27:09.827 --> 01:27:11.160
this is a really cool invention.

01:27:11.160 --> 01:27:12.550
This should really
be focused on us.

01:27:12.550 --> 01:27:14.190
And so when the
Lumiere operators would

01:27:14.190 --> 01:27:15.310
come, for example,
they'd be like,

01:27:15.310 --> 01:27:16.740
well, you should really
do the coronation.

01:27:16.740 --> 01:27:18.448
And the operators were
getting like, this

01:27:18.448 --> 01:27:22.080
is like the 35th
coronation we've filmed.

01:27:22.080 --> 01:27:25.698
So you had this kind of process
where elite members of society

01:27:25.698 --> 01:27:27.990
we're like, well, we're really
the appropriate subjects

01:27:27.990 --> 01:27:28.490
for this.

01:27:28.490 --> 01:27:31.230
But as you're both saying,
that at the very early stuff

01:27:31.230 --> 01:27:33.000
is just this kind of daily life.

01:27:33.000 --> 01:27:35.250
And so one of the things
that's interesting about this

01:27:35.250 --> 01:27:37.680
is that some of the
very earliest films,

01:27:37.680 --> 01:27:39.930
what documentary film
folks like to say,

01:27:39.930 --> 01:27:42.330
is that some of the very
earliest films that were made

01:27:42.330 --> 01:27:45.480
were actually what we might
think of as documentaries,

01:27:45.480 --> 01:27:47.905
or actualities as they
refer to them at the time.

01:27:47.905 --> 01:27:50.280
And one of the things that's
always really striking to me

01:27:50.280 --> 01:27:54.447
is that the sense of wonder.

01:27:54.447 --> 01:27:56.280
But like, wow, look at
this new contraption.

01:27:56.280 --> 01:27:57.900
Look what it can do.

01:27:57.900 --> 01:28:00.690
Look how we can see
the world differently

01:28:00.690 --> 01:28:02.670
through using this machine.

01:28:02.670 --> 01:28:04.590
And look at how we
can look at ourselves.

01:28:04.590 --> 01:28:07.440
And you get this kind of
sense of excitement and fun

01:28:07.440 --> 01:28:10.020
about this new way of
looking through the world

01:28:10.020 --> 01:28:12.340
through this technology.

01:28:12.340 --> 01:28:14.730
And I think that was something
that's very powerful early

01:28:14.730 --> 01:28:15.990
on in that and that we--

01:28:18.690 --> 01:28:20.310
important to think
about in terms

01:28:20.310 --> 01:28:23.172
of these kinds of technologies
and how people related to them.

01:28:23.172 --> 01:28:25.380
But now we're going to look
at another kind of camera

01:28:25.380 --> 01:28:27.252
that was a very different
kind of invention,

01:28:27.252 --> 01:28:29.460
and what were the implications
of this different kind

01:28:29.460 --> 01:28:31.095
of invention for what got made.

01:28:36.640 --> 01:28:41.380
OK, so at the same time or--

01:28:41.380 --> 01:28:44.380
almost exactly the same
time, Thomas Edison

01:28:44.380 --> 01:28:50.230
was also working
on moving images.

01:28:50.230 --> 01:28:54.310
And he invented the
kinetoscope in 1893.

01:28:54.310 --> 01:28:56.390
And in contrast to
the cinematograph,

01:28:56.390 --> 01:28:59.470
the kinetoscope weighed
over 1,000 pounds,

01:28:59.470 --> 01:29:03.430
it required electricity, and
so it was impossible to move.

01:29:03.430 --> 01:29:06.730
Basically it stayed
inside a studio at Edison

01:29:06.730 --> 01:29:09.842
in East Orange, New Jersey
called the Black Mariah,

01:29:09.842 --> 01:29:11.050
so you have pictures of the--

01:29:11.050 --> 01:29:12.250
CHRIS BOEBEL: Which
is on the left there.

01:29:12.250 --> 01:29:13.870
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Mariah there--

01:29:13.870 --> 01:29:17.642
And because this camera was
so big and heavy and unwieldy

01:29:17.642 --> 01:29:18.850
and it worked on electricity.

01:29:18.850 --> 01:29:20.920
And of course Edison wanted
to work on electricity

01:29:20.920 --> 01:29:21.820
because that was his invention.

01:29:21.820 --> 01:29:23.570
So it was partly,
again, business concerns

01:29:23.570 --> 01:29:28.510
were also central in terms
of how the technology got

01:29:28.510 --> 01:29:30.200
formulated or it got used.

01:29:30.200 --> 01:29:32.283
So instead of the camera
going out into the world,

01:29:32.283 --> 01:29:33.658
it's not something
you can really

01:29:33.658 --> 01:29:34.960
do with an invention like this.

01:29:34.960 --> 01:29:37.460
So what do you do instead?

01:29:37.460 --> 01:29:39.970
You bring things to the camera.

01:29:39.970 --> 01:29:42.412
So what do you
bring to the camera?

01:29:42.412 --> 01:29:43.120
AUDIENCE: People.

01:29:43.120 --> 01:29:45.043
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
People doing what?

01:29:45.043 --> 01:29:47.710
So if you're inside this kind of
studio, this big, black studio,

01:29:47.710 --> 01:29:49.460
and you've got this
camera that sits still

01:29:49.460 --> 01:29:52.880
and that runs on electricity--

01:29:52.880 --> 01:29:55.490
performers, vaudeville.

01:29:55.490 --> 01:29:58.280
So you think about what
was going on in terms

01:29:58.280 --> 01:30:01.730
of entertainment at the time--

01:30:01.730 --> 01:30:06.260
things like vaudeville, street
performers, circus acts,

01:30:06.260 --> 01:30:09.140
often being brought
in front of--

01:30:09.140 --> 01:30:11.930
to perform within the studio.

01:30:11.930 --> 01:30:12.860
And that being filmed.

01:30:12.860 --> 01:30:14.690
And that and that being--

01:30:14.690 --> 01:30:18.110
moving it already more in terms
of the entertainment model,

01:30:18.110 --> 01:30:20.615
for example, rather
than more what

01:30:20.615 --> 01:30:22.490
we would think of as in
the documentary model

01:30:22.490 --> 01:30:23.790
with the Lumiere camera.

01:30:23.790 --> 01:30:26.630
So just to show you a few
clips of the Edison films--

01:30:26.630 --> 01:30:28.220
CHRIS BOEBEL: I just want to--

01:30:28.220 --> 01:30:29.840
I want to make one point.

01:30:29.840 --> 01:30:33.710
Just in terms of how the
technology that you're using

01:30:33.710 --> 01:30:37.250
influences both the outcome,
as Chris has mentioned,

01:30:37.250 --> 01:30:38.690
but also the experience.

01:30:38.690 --> 01:30:40.910
Just imagine the
difference between being

01:30:40.910 --> 01:30:45.770
the person here and that
person, as a filmmaker.

01:30:45.770 --> 01:30:49.610
This person is out in the
open, in the sunlight,

01:30:49.610 --> 01:30:54.050
finding their subject, children
playing, trains going by,

01:30:54.050 --> 01:30:57.080
people walking
around, walls falling.

01:30:57.080 --> 01:30:59.690
There's this sense of
freedom and experience

01:30:59.690 --> 01:31:01.220
and engagement with the world.

01:31:01.220 --> 01:31:04.070
That person is sitting
in a black room.

01:31:04.070 --> 01:31:08.880
And people on a tight
schedule are coming to him.

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:11.450
And that is a really
great encapsulation

01:31:11.450 --> 01:31:17.060
of a dichotomy that
continues to this day.

01:31:17.060 --> 01:31:20.000
This is two different
ways of making films.

01:31:20.000 --> 01:31:23.305
And so now should we
look at an Edison?

01:31:23.305 --> 01:31:25.430
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So the
serpentine dances and then

01:31:25.430 --> 01:31:27.860
the strong man was the
one that I had marked.

01:32:25.340 --> 01:32:27.090
OK, we can watch another
one just for fun.

01:32:53.560 --> 01:32:56.060
OK.

01:32:56.060 --> 01:32:58.240
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
you want to see more?

01:32:58.240 --> 01:32:58.720
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Unless you guys--

01:32:58.720 --> 01:33:00.310
you want to see another one?

01:33:00.310 --> 01:33:01.400
For fun?

01:33:01.400 --> 01:33:01.900
OK.

01:33:40.080 --> 01:33:45.540
OK, so comments,
reactions on those?

01:33:45.540 --> 01:33:48.120
AUDIENCE: It almost
seemed like as a--

01:33:48.120 --> 01:33:50.970
you could only see
each individual frame.

01:33:50.970 --> 01:33:53.740
It was almost like you
took like a strobe light

01:33:53.740 --> 01:33:55.240
and it was recording
with the strobe

01:33:55.240 --> 01:33:56.282
light flashing like that.

01:33:56.282 --> 01:33:58.365
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, the
frame rate is much higher.

01:33:58.365 --> 01:34:01.180
That's why it's all a little bit
slow motion if it's played back

01:34:01.180 --> 01:34:02.650
at 24 frames a second.

01:34:02.650 --> 01:34:05.055
But yeah, it's
artificial light, much

01:34:05.055 --> 01:34:07.180
higher frame rate, a much
more constant frame rate.

01:34:07.180 --> 01:34:08.440
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Do you want to
explain what the frame rate is?

01:34:08.440 --> 01:34:10.450
CHRIS BOEBEL: Oh, so
frames per second,

01:34:10.450 --> 01:34:13.450
like how many frames go through
the camera in a given second.

01:34:13.450 --> 01:34:17.350
So the standard for video is--

01:34:17.350 --> 01:34:21.160
well there are several standards
now-- it's like 24 or 30.

01:34:21.160 --> 01:34:25.540
Standard film, old
film technology is 24.

01:34:25.540 --> 01:34:28.970
The Lumiere shorts are
not a constant frame rate

01:34:28.970 --> 01:34:30.970
because it depends on the
skill of the operator.

01:34:30.970 --> 01:34:34.870
If you are cranking at a
very, very smooth rate,

01:34:34.870 --> 01:34:40.630
you might get it to be about 18
or 20 or something like that.

01:34:40.630 --> 01:34:43.690
Edison, of course, it's
electric, so it's very constant

01:34:43.690 --> 01:34:47.380
and I think a higher
frame rate than what

01:34:47.380 --> 01:34:49.780
it's being played back at in
these shorts, which is why

01:34:49.780 --> 01:34:51.190
it has that slow motion feel.

01:34:51.190 --> 01:34:54.520
But yeah, exactly,
it's got probably--

01:34:54.520 --> 01:34:56.320
the strobe is the
fact that there's

01:34:56.320 --> 01:34:59.350
a shutter on the camera that's
a very even shutter that's

01:34:59.350 --> 01:35:00.880
going at a constant rate.

01:35:00.880 --> 01:35:03.520
And the light is-- it
took a lot of light

01:35:03.520 --> 01:35:07.600
to make these, which is
massive, massive banks of lights

01:35:07.600 --> 01:35:12.700
inside the studio, as opposed to
the daylight and the hand crank

01:35:12.700 --> 01:35:15.910
and very different feel.

01:35:15.910 --> 01:35:17.327
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Other reactions?

01:35:17.327 --> 01:35:19.035
AUDIENCE: I had very
obvious this comment

01:35:19.035 --> 01:35:20.650
that compared to the
other films where

01:35:20.650 --> 01:35:22.570
it will come as portable,
instead of having

01:35:22.570 --> 01:35:25.030
the camera going after the
people and chasing them,

01:35:25.030 --> 01:35:27.670
you have people kind
of trying to stay

01:35:27.670 --> 01:35:29.498
within the frame
dictated by the camera.

01:35:29.498 --> 01:35:31.540
So the dancer is doing
all these wonderful moves,

01:35:31.540 --> 01:35:33.940
but is basically standing
on the same spot.

01:35:33.940 --> 01:35:36.410
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
it's studio filmmaking,

01:35:36.410 --> 01:35:40.720
which again, persists, of
course, to the present day.

01:35:40.720 --> 01:35:43.120
But it's completely controlled.

01:35:43.120 --> 01:35:46.510
It's in a completely
artificial environment.

01:35:46.510 --> 01:35:49.720
And they're told
where to stand, what

01:35:49.720 --> 01:35:54.340
to do, start, stop, action,
cut, let's do another take.

01:35:54.340 --> 01:35:58.780
I mean, it's very much what
we think of as mainstream,

01:35:58.780 --> 01:36:00.160
commercial filmmaking.

01:36:00.160 --> 01:36:02.800
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And so what
else about content, in addition

01:36:02.800 --> 01:36:06.970
to being entertainment,
what kinds of entertainment?

01:36:09.730 --> 01:36:13.690
I mean one thing to be thinking
about during this period-- what

01:36:13.690 --> 01:36:16.840
was popular entertainment
like in the 1890s?

01:36:16.840 --> 01:36:22.870
A lot of it was geared
towards working class men

01:36:22.870 --> 01:36:23.990
in urban areas.

01:36:23.990 --> 01:36:25.660
So that's partly--
the cockfights,

01:36:25.660 --> 01:36:26.690
the fighting matches.

01:36:26.690 --> 01:36:32.158
I mean so this was part of
popular culture in urban areas.

01:36:32.158 --> 01:36:33.700
Particularly, this
is also the period

01:36:33.700 --> 01:36:35.020
where you have massive
industrialization,

01:36:35.020 --> 01:36:37.360
so you have a lot of factory
workers who are moving in,

01:36:37.360 --> 01:36:40.330
and who have money because
they're earning wages,

01:36:40.330 --> 01:36:43.060
where they can have
money to go and pay

01:36:43.060 --> 01:36:44.950
to go watch these things.

01:36:44.950 --> 01:36:47.950
So it's built into
that entertainment,

01:36:47.950 --> 01:36:53.140
popular entertainment for a
growing population of people

01:36:53.140 --> 01:36:54.718
who are earning
wages in factories,

01:36:54.718 --> 01:36:56.260
for example, and in
this early stuff,

01:36:56.260 --> 01:36:58.390
particularly for the men.

01:36:58.390 --> 01:37:01.100
And then you also have
the circus stuff as well.

01:37:01.100 --> 01:37:02.872
Whereas the Lumiere
stuff, the Lumieres

01:37:02.872 --> 01:37:05.080
were coming from a much more
middle class background.

01:37:05.080 --> 01:37:08.890
There's so much more middle
class sensibility to--

01:37:08.890 --> 01:37:11.050
I mean, you see the factory
workers and the factory

01:37:11.050 --> 01:37:14.800
too at the same time, but
a somewhat different kind

01:37:14.800 --> 01:37:16.940
of feel in terms of focus,
in terms of content.

01:37:16.940 --> 01:37:19.000
So again, thinking
about, well, what's

01:37:19.000 --> 01:37:21.615
the social context in which
these things are being made?

01:37:21.615 --> 01:37:23.740
As I talked about earlier,
that's one of the things

01:37:23.740 --> 01:37:26.080
we always want to
be focusing on,

01:37:26.080 --> 01:37:30.770
the social embedding of the kind
of images that are being made.

01:37:30.770 --> 01:37:32.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: So let's--

01:37:32.320 --> 01:37:33.487
so we'll pause.

01:37:33.487 --> 01:37:34.070
Let's take a--

01:37:34.070 --> 01:37:34.570
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
That's the end of this.

01:37:34.570 --> 01:37:35.650
CHRIS BOEBEL: --a tiny break.

01:37:35.650 --> 01:37:36.760
And then we can come back.

01:37:36.760 --> 01:37:38.385
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Well, and just to let

01:37:38.385 --> 01:37:40.930
what we're going to do after
this couple minute break,

01:37:40.930 --> 01:37:45.400
is show you some of the very
earliest clips from science

01:37:45.400 --> 01:37:49.640
documentaries again, which are
I think are quite fun and quite

01:37:49.640 --> 01:37:50.140
interesting.

01:37:50.140 --> 01:37:51.880
And we'll just talk
about them very briefly.

01:37:51.880 --> 01:37:54.505
And then we're just going to go
over the syllabus for the class

01:37:54.505 --> 01:37:58.060
and explain more about the
mechanics of how it works.

01:37:58.060 --> 01:38:02.470
OK, so just to look at a
couple of the very earliest,

01:38:02.470 --> 01:38:04.960
what now gets referred to
as the earliest science

01:38:04.960 --> 01:38:07.030
documentaries-- and if
any of you are interested,

01:38:07.030 --> 01:38:07.795
I did put a--

01:38:07.795 --> 01:38:10.060
I mean obviously
you weren't expected

01:38:10.060 --> 01:38:12.250
to do any reading today
before you got the syllabus.

01:38:12.250 --> 01:38:14.120
But if you are
interested in this topic,

01:38:14.120 --> 01:38:17.200
there's an article for today
by Hannah Landecker that

01:38:17.200 --> 01:38:19.300
talks a little bit about
these very early science

01:38:19.300 --> 01:38:20.982
documentaries and
why say scientists

01:38:20.982 --> 01:38:22.190
thought they were interested.

01:38:22.190 --> 01:38:23.980
So if you're interested in
that, it's really short.

01:38:23.980 --> 01:38:25.272
It's like seven or eight pages.

01:38:25.272 --> 01:38:29.080
So if you're interested,
there's information there

01:38:29.080 --> 01:38:32.890
if you'd like to find out more.

01:38:32.890 --> 01:38:34.700
OK, so the early 20th
century-- so again,

01:38:34.700 --> 01:38:36.283
just a few years
later from the period

01:38:36.283 --> 01:38:38.060
that we were talking about.

01:38:38.060 --> 01:38:43.390
So say the first 10 years
or so, the next decade,

01:38:43.390 --> 01:38:50.230
you also see in addition to
the kind of Lumiere shorts

01:38:50.230 --> 01:38:52.300
that you saw, and then
you see the Edison stuff

01:38:52.300 --> 01:38:54.610
across the ocean, happening
in the United States,

01:38:54.610 --> 01:38:58.060
you also had this other
subgenres starting to emerge.

01:38:58.060 --> 01:39:00.520
And one of the very early
subgenres that you start seeing

01:39:00.520 --> 01:39:03.490
is an interest in science films
about-- these little films

01:39:03.490 --> 01:39:06.280
about science, which are
interesting from a number

01:39:06.280 --> 01:39:08.270
of reasons that we're
going to talk about.

01:39:08.270 --> 01:39:10.690
And one thing to keep in mind
is if you go back and think

01:39:10.690 --> 01:39:12.555
to the 19th century--

01:39:12.555 --> 01:39:13.930
now I'm trying to
think of what's

01:39:13.930 --> 01:39:15.472
the big Hollywood
movie that was done

01:39:15.472 --> 01:39:21.445
about natural history in the--

01:39:21.445 --> 01:39:23.320
I'm trying to think of
a Hollywood movie that

01:39:23.320 --> 01:39:26.020
might convey some of the
interest in natural history

01:39:26.020 --> 01:39:29.200
that was so prevalent in
the late 19th century.

01:39:29.200 --> 01:39:33.510
Insects and-- it's
not ringing a bell.

01:39:33.510 --> 01:39:34.760
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:39:34.760 --> 01:39:35.850
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
No, you've seen it.

01:39:35.850 --> 01:39:36.642
We saw it together.

01:39:36.642 --> 01:39:39.060
So I'm just misremembering the--

01:39:39.060 --> 01:39:40.560
not being able to
remember the name.

01:39:40.560 --> 01:39:43.020
But if you think back
to the 19th century,

01:39:43.020 --> 01:39:45.520
there was a lot of interest in
natural history, for example.

01:39:45.520 --> 01:39:48.810
So popular activities, say for
a lot of middle class families,

01:39:48.810 --> 01:39:53.820
might be going out and
finding various insects,

01:39:53.820 --> 01:39:56.220
and making displays,
and pinning them down,

01:39:56.220 --> 01:40:01.290
and doing natural
history drawings of birds

01:40:01.290 --> 01:40:03.088
and other kinds of
wildlife, for example.

01:40:03.088 --> 01:40:05.130
So there was a lot of
there was a lot of interest

01:40:05.130 --> 01:40:08.160
in natural history in
the late 19th century.

01:40:08.160 --> 01:40:13.382
And that being part of a broader
popular interest in science

01:40:13.382 --> 01:40:15.090
that was quite prevalent
during this time

01:40:15.090 --> 01:40:17.507
period, the late 19th century
into the early 20th century.

01:40:17.507 --> 01:40:21.450
So there's a lot of popular
interest in science,

01:40:21.450 --> 01:40:26.170
and the natural history model
being one strong piece of that.

01:40:26.170 --> 01:40:27.600
And so the very
early-- what often

01:40:27.600 --> 01:40:31.410
gets referred to as the
very first science film

01:40:31.410 --> 01:40:33.660
is a short film
called Cheese Mites.

01:40:33.660 --> 01:40:37.680
And it was shown at the Alhambra
Theater in London in 1903.

01:40:37.680 --> 01:40:39.230
And it was organized
by a businessman

01:40:39.230 --> 01:40:40.890
named Charles Urban.

01:40:40.890 --> 01:40:42.720
And it was shown as
part of a program.

01:40:42.720 --> 01:40:45.240
It also had other popular
entertainment acts and music

01:40:45.240 --> 01:40:46.690
on the stage.

01:40:46.690 --> 01:40:50.280
And so we're just going
to show you Cheese Mites.

01:40:50.280 --> 01:40:53.700
So Cheese Mites is looking
through a microscope

01:40:53.700 --> 01:41:02.020
and filming what you see looking
through a microscope on cheese,

01:41:02.020 --> 01:41:03.220
microorganisms on cheese.

01:41:49.370 --> 01:41:54.010
OK, so here some of
this very early film

01:41:54.010 --> 01:41:55.760
footage of these
microorganisms on cheese.

01:41:55.760 --> 01:41:58.850
And then when it was
shown in its theater form,

01:41:58.850 --> 01:42:03.410
eventually they added a little
piece at the beginning of it,

01:42:03.410 --> 01:42:05.605
where you see a man kind
of dressed in formal

01:42:05.605 --> 01:42:06.980
or sitting down
to eat his lunch.

01:42:06.980 --> 01:42:08.390
And he's going to have
some Stilton cheese.

01:42:08.390 --> 01:42:09.920
And he looks down at
the Stilton cheese.

01:42:09.920 --> 01:42:11.962
And then you look through
the eye of a microscope

01:42:11.962 --> 01:42:15.110
and you see all these
microorganisms on the cheese.

01:42:15.110 --> 01:42:19.683
And he's like aghast at
what's on this cheese.

01:42:19.683 --> 01:42:21.350
So the idea of like
to get the audiences

01:42:21.350 --> 01:42:25.710
excited about thinking
about these microorganisms--

01:42:25.710 --> 01:42:27.260
So any reactions to this?

01:42:27.260 --> 01:42:31.653
Any things that you guys
thought was interesting?

01:42:31.653 --> 01:42:34.070
When it was shown in theaters,
it would have been played--

01:42:34.070 --> 01:42:37.768
music would have been playing
as part of the entertainment.

01:42:37.768 --> 01:42:39.560
CHRIS BOEBEL: But the
original version is--

01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:40.670
CHRISTINE WALLEY: --is silent.

01:42:40.670 --> 01:42:43.087
CHRIS BOEBEL: And that's what--
we saw it in its entirety,

01:42:43.087 --> 01:42:46.880
I mean it's just a shot
through a microscope.

01:42:46.880 --> 01:42:49.730
AUDIENCE: How long
was the original one?

01:42:49.730 --> 01:42:50.710
Was incredibly long?

01:42:50.710 --> 01:42:51.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY: That was it.

01:42:51.960 --> 01:42:53.690
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Yeah, just a couple

01:42:53.690 --> 01:42:57.290
of minutes, whatever they
had on a short reel of film.

01:43:00.610 --> 01:43:03.220
Yeah, no, I mean, who knows how
long it took them to make it

01:43:03.220 --> 01:43:06.040
and how many outtakes there
were, probably a lot, probably

01:43:06.040 --> 01:43:07.690
a lot.

01:43:07.690 --> 01:43:10.240
I mean, to me it's
interesting because it's

01:43:10.240 --> 01:43:15.460
the equivalent of the IMAX
movie, nature film of today,

01:43:15.460 --> 01:43:17.350
or the Discovery Channel.

01:43:17.350 --> 01:43:22.630
It's about taking the audience
somewhere completely new

01:43:22.630 --> 01:43:26.320
and outside of their
experience in the natural world

01:43:26.320 --> 01:43:31.100
and revealing something,
something previously unknown.

01:43:31.100 --> 01:43:34.730
So it seems relatively crude.

01:43:34.730 --> 01:43:37.160
But it's fulfilling
the same function.

01:43:37.160 --> 01:43:39.520
And I think
audiences went crazy.

01:43:39.520 --> 01:43:40.250
They loved it.

01:43:40.250 --> 01:43:41.200
It was a huge hit.

01:43:41.200 --> 01:43:41.680
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
That was the question

01:43:41.680 --> 01:43:42.847
that I was going to ask you.

01:43:42.847 --> 01:43:44.530
Why do you think they
would have found--

01:43:44.530 --> 01:43:46.947
I mean why would this have
been so interesting in the time

01:43:46.947 --> 01:43:48.245
period?

01:43:48.245 --> 01:43:49.620
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
some things

01:43:49.620 --> 01:43:51.370
you can't see to yourself.

01:43:51.370 --> 01:43:54.370
But like if you know some other
films like they kind of show

01:43:54.370 --> 01:43:56.620
the whole, like we had
to tell the subjects

01:43:56.620 --> 01:43:59.800
versus the [INAUDIBLE].

01:43:59.800 --> 01:44:02.020
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
and actually, so again,

01:44:02.020 --> 01:44:05.958
this the kind of excitement
around film technology

01:44:05.958 --> 01:44:07.750
during this period,
because it's like, wow.

01:44:07.750 --> 01:44:09.680
It's like this whole new
way of seeing the world.

01:44:09.680 --> 01:44:11.440
And one of the things that
was really exciting here--

01:44:11.440 --> 01:44:13.780
and this was not only exciting
for popular audiences,

01:44:13.780 --> 01:44:15.520
it was also exciting
for scientists--

01:44:15.520 --> 01:44:17.650
was the fact that you
could see things--

01:44:17.650 --> 01:44:19.720
I mean through a microscope,
you could see things

01:44:19.720 --> 01:44:20.900
on a really small scale.

01:44:20.900 --> 01:44:23.710
But here could see them moving
on a really small scale.

01:44:23.710 --> 01:44:26.560
And what Hannah Landecker
argues in her article

01:44:26.560 --> 01:44:30.100
about this is that this was
really exciting for scientists

01:44:30.100 --> 01:44:33.640
also at the time, being able
to see things in this way,

01:44:33.640 --> 01:44:39.640
because for them, it really
emphasized the power of process

01:44:39.640 --> 01:44:42.400
or the fact that things
were always in motion,

01:44:42.400 --> 01:44:43.720
they were always moving.

01:44:43.720 --> 01:44:45.700
And she talks about
how at the time,

01:44:45.700 --> 01:44:49.670
it was talked about, well,
if you had a lot of--

01:44:49.670 --> 01:44:51.430
in science textbooks,
you might have

01:44:51.430 --> 01:44:54.040
a lot of drawings of biological
organisms, for example.

01:44:54.040 --> 01:44:55.480
But they were static drawings.

01:44:55.480 --> 01:44:58.480
And even if you had photographs,
the photographs were static.

01:44:58.480 --> 01:45:00.160
So you might be
talking about something

01:45:00.160 --> 01:45:02.860
like reproduction
of microorganisms

01:45:02.860 --> 01:45:05.920
or whatever it might be.

01:45:05.920 --> 01:45:09.168
And you might-- obviously you
know that that's a process.

01:45:09.168 --> 01:45:10.960
But it doesn't really
hit you over the head

01:45:10.960 --> 01:45:13.510
in the same kind of way
as when you see organisms

01:45:13.510 --> 01:45:14.900
actually moving all the time.

01:45:14.900 --> 01:45:17.170
And so, according to her,
that this was something

01:45:17.170 --> 01:45:19.840
that was a real revelation
for science at the time,

01:45:19.840 --> 01:45:22.330
about oh, wow, things
are always in motion,

01:45:22.330 --> 01:45:24.070
they're always moving.

01:45:24.070 --> 01:45:25.570
And so there was a
lot of excitement

01:45:25.570 --> 01:45:28.400
about what can we see
through this new technology.

01:45:28.400 --> 01:45:29.320
And one of the things
that's interesting

01:45:29.320 --> 01:45:31.153
that she talks about,
and a number of others

01:45:31.153 --> 01:45:34.960
talk about also, is that
for these very early pieces,

01:45:34.960 --> 01:45:36.790
you might have
scientists be excited

01:45:36.790 --> 01:45:39.610
about the same kinds of
images that might then later

01:45:39.610 --> 01:45:40.603
be shown in theaters.

01:45:40.603 --> 01:45:42.520
So the bit that we just
saw, Cheese Mites, you

01:45:42.520 --> 01:45:47.943
might have scientists watching
these images at conferences,

01:45:47.943 --> 01:45:49.360
thinking about
their implications,

01:45:49.360 --> 01:45:51.693
or does it suggest different
kinds of experiments to do,

01:45:51.693 --> 01:45:52.810
for example.

01:45:52.810 --> 01:45:54.760
And then you put the
bit about the guy

01:45:54.760 --> 01:45:57.010
eating his Stilton lunch for
cheese, and being aghast.

01:45:57.010 --> 01:45:58.990
And you put music to it and
you show it in the theater.

01:45:58.990 --> 01:46:00.740
And it might be the
same exact same images

01:46:00.740 --> 01:46:03.250
being shown for popular
audiences, which

01:46:03.250 --> 01:46:04.240
is quite interesting.

01:46:04.240 --> 01:46:06.100
But to show you a few
more of the early ones

01:46:06.100 --> 01:46:07.600
and what people
were interested in--

01:46:07.600 --> 01:46:09.892
CHRIS BOEBEL: Do you want to
see with sound or without?

01:46:09.892 --> 01:46:12.543
It has that kind of
narration, narrative.

01:46:12.543 --> 01:46:13.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Because we don't

01:46:13.960 --> 01:46:15.760
have just the
images without any--

01:46:15.760 --> 01:46:17.840
we have all about the
people stuff in between.

01:46:17.840 --> 01:46:19.090
Should we do it without sound?

01:46:19.090 --> 01:46:20.173
Let's do it without sound.

01:46:20.173 --> 01:46:22.070
CHRIS BOEBEL: The problem
is then, it makes--

01:46:22.070 --> 01:46:24.070
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK,
then do it with the sound.

01:46:24.070 --> 01:46:24.400
That's fine.

01:46:24.400 --> 01:46:26.817
CHRIS BOEBEL: So this is a
little documentary put together

01:46:26.817 --> 01:46:28.540
by New Scientist.

01:46:31.123 --> 01:46:31.790
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:46:31.790 --> 01:46:34.670
- In August, 1903, this
film made its debut

01:46:34.670 --> 01:46:37.340
at the Alhambra
Theater in London.

01:46:37.340 --> 01:46:39.470
Its stars are cheese
mites crawling

01:46:39.470 --> 01:46:40.940
through a piece of Stilton.

01:46:40.940 --> 01:46:44.300
But on the big screen, they look
more like eight-legged aliens.

01:46:44.300 --> 01:46:47.090
It was an instant hit and
marked the beginning of science

01:46:47.090 --> 01:46:49.100
in nature filmmaking.

01:46:49.100 --> 01:46:52.700
- The original film
was just a minute long

01:46:52.700 --> 01:46:56.240
and it just shows cheese mites
shot down the microscope.

01:46:56.240 --> 01:46:59.390
There has been a story
put about that the cheese

01:46:59.390 --> 01:47:03.230
manufacturers complained
about the cheese mites film.

01:47:03.230 --> 01:47:06.050
- Cheese Mites was produced
by Francis Martin Duncan,

01:47:06.050 --> 01:47:09.050
an amateur natural historian
who previously published books

01:47:09.050 --> 01:47:11.330
on how to take photos
through microscopes.

01:47:11.330 --> 01:47:13.610
Another nature enthusiast
called Percy Smith

01:47:13.610 --> 01:47:15.950
followed in his footsteps,
and made a film called

01:47:15.950 --> 01:47:19.060
The Birth of a Flower in 1910.

01:47:19.060 --> 01:47:21.410
- Percy Smith at the time
was working as a clerk

01:47:21.410 --> 01:47:23.540
in the board of education.

01:47:23.540 --> 01:47:27.960
And he fixed up a special
filming set up in his bathroom.

01:47:27.960 --> 01:47:29.630
He could darken the room.

01:47:29.630 --> 01:47:32.330
And he made a [? Jim ?]
crack device made up

01:47:32.330 --> 01:47:36.350
of a seesaw with
two old tin cans.

01:47:36.350 --> 01:47:38.930
The tin cans sort of
filled up with water.

01:47:38.930 --> 01:47:41.570
And when it would reach
the bottom, clunk,

01:47:41.570 --> 01:47:43.500
it fired the shutter
of the camera.

01:47:43.500 --> 01:47:47.180
And using this extraordinary
homemade piece of apparatus,

01:47:47.180 --> 01:47:49.970
Percy Smith made the
very beautiful film,

01:47:49.970 --> 01:47:51.940
Birth of a Flower.

01:47:51.940 --> 01:47:54.310
- Since film reels were
only one minute long,

01:47:54.310 --> 01:47:57.310
filmmakers were limited
to making short films.

01:47:57.310 --> 01:48:00.400
But these were shared alongside
other musical variety acts

01:48:00.400 --> 01:48:02.290
that were typical at the time.

01:48:02.290 --> 01:48:04.570
People went to the theater
simply to be amazed.

01:48:04.570 --> 01:48:07.090
And that's just how they
reacted to Smith's next film

01:48:07.090 --> 01:48:08.260
of a juggling flying.

01:48:08.260 --> 01:48:11.535
- The Acrobatic Fly was
a bit of close-up film,

01:48:11.535 --> 01:48:13.810
[INAUDIBLE] shot
through a microscope.

01:48:13.810 --> 01:48:17.170
And what Percy did was
to tie the fly down

01:48:17.170 --> 01:48:19.990
with a tiny piece
of silk thread,

01:48:19.990 --> 01:48:24.010
and then just pass small
objects to the fly.

01:48:24.010 --> 01:48:25.990
It looked as though
the fly was juggling.

01:48:25.990 --> 01:48:27.970
And that's what the public saw.

01:48:27.970 --> 01:48:30.700
But in actual fact, Percy
came to the conclusion,

01:48:30.700 --> 01:48:32.770
the fly was just doing
what came naturally to it,

01:48:32.770 --> 01:48:34.640
ideally trying to walk.

01:48:34.640 --> 01:48:38.710
So when he gave the fly a
little ball made of pith,

01:48:38.710 --> 01:48:43.260
for example, then the fly
walked and the ball went round.

01:48:43.260 --> 01:48:45.160
And in the best
shot of the film,

01:48:45.160 --> 01:48:47.110
there's a ball with
a fly underneath

01:48:47.110 --> 01:48:49.300
and another side walking on top.

01:48:49.300 --> 01:48:52.990
Spectacular, people loved it.

01:48:52.990 --> 01:48:56.050
- It soon became clear that
the public loved nature films.

01:48:56.050 --> 01:48:58.660
And the trend was
linked to Britain.

01:48:58.660 --> 01:48:59.320
In America--

01:48:59.320 --> 01:48:59.903
[END PLAYBACK]

01:48:59.903 --> 01:49:02.607
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK we can--

01:49:02.607 --> 01:49:03.190
that's enough.

01:49:06.770 --> 01:49:07.840
Reactions, comments?

01:49:07.840 --> 01:49:11.140
CHRIS BOEBEL: Who said
science isn't entertaining?

01:49:11.140 --> 01:49:13.630
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Reactions, comments?

01:49:13.630 --> 01:49:16.630
AUDIENCE: Interesting
ethic of the documentary,

01:49:16.630 --> 01:49:18.980
making a tie a fly with a rope.

01:49:18.980 --> 01:49:20.990
You don't tell anything
to the audience.

01:49:20.990 --> 01:49:22.360
Nobody cares.

01:49:22.360 --> 01:49:25.390
Now we wouldn't be able
to do the same, right?

01:49:25.390 --> 01:49:28.330
CHRIS BOEBEL: The cockfighting
film, the Thomas Edison-- yeah.

01:49:28.330 --> 01:49:29.500
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
again, at this period

01:49:29.500 --> 01:49:32.140
before this distinction between
fiction and documentary film,

01:49:32.140 --> 01:49:37.810
that distinction doesn't really
solidify until around 1930.

01:49:37.810 --> 01:49:41.560
Although, certainly there's more
debate about different kinds

01:49:41.560 --> 01:49:42.670
of genres before then.

01:49:42.670 --> 01:49:45.035
But here, yeah, kind of
a blending of genres,

01:49:45.035 --> 01:49:47.410
and again, sort of the science
film and the entertainment

01:49:47.410 --> 01:49:49.285
film, and not necessarily
at this point there

01:49:49.285 --> 01:49:51.370
being a strong distinction.

01:49:51.370 --> 01:49:54.483
I mean, well, certainly the
fly with the little mud balls,

01:49:54.483 --> 01:49:56.650
I don't know how many
scientists were watching that.

01:49:56.650 --> 01:49:59.950
But with Cheese Mites,
they might be, for example.

01:49:59.950 --> 01:50:01.800
So again, not a really
strong distinction.

01:50:01.800 --> 01:50:03.970
And the time lapse photography,
that's another one.

01:50:03.970 --> 01:50:05.512
I mean back to this
question of, what

01:50:05.512 --> 01:50:07.180
would have been
exciting at the time?

01:50:07.180 --> 01:50:10.390
How can you see the world
differently or in a new way,

01:50:10.390 --> 01:50:13.690
or through new eyes using
this kind of technology?

01:50:13.690 --> 01:50:16.900
And this is actually a lot of
what Hannah Landecker focuses

01:50:16.900 --> 01:50:20.050
on was that this early use
of time lapse photography,

01:50:20.050 --> 01:50:25.420
where you would take a little
bit, maybe every seven minutes.

01:50:25.420 --> 01:50:28.390
And through doing that,
you get a new sense

01:50:28.390 --> 01:50:31.420
of development of
plants, for example,

01:50:31.420 --> 01:50:33.670
because if you're just
watching it with the naked eye,

01:50:33.670 --> 01:50:35.087
just observing
with the naked eye,

01:50:35.087 --> 01:50:38.350
you're going to fall
asleep before you really

01:50:38.350 --> 01:50:39.310
watch much happening.

01:50:39.310 --> 01:50:40.990
And it's hard for people
to concentrate and pay

01:50:40.990 --> 01:50:42.170
attention for that long.

01:50:42.170 --> 01:50:45.130
But if you use time
lapse photography,

01:50:45.130 --> 01:50:47.500
you get this appreciation
for like, oh, wow, here's

01:50:47.500 --> 01:50:48.100
the process.

01:50:48.100 --> 01:50:49.040
Here's the movement.

01:50:49.040 --> 01:50:49.910
This is how it goes.

01:50:49.910 --> 01:50:53.350
And again, placing more
emphasis on movement and motion

01:50:53.350 --> 01:50:57.297
and process, and
getting scientists

01:50:57.297 --> 01:50:59.630
excited about thinking about
it in those kinds of terms.

01:50:59.630 --> 01:51:04.018
So again, the power
of cinema cameras

01:51:04.018 --> 01:51:06.560
to get people thinking about
the world and new kinds of ways,

01:51:06.560 --> 01:51:07.540
whether it's through
entertainment,

01:51:07.540 --> 01:51:10.120
or whether it's through science
or whatever it might be--

01:51:10.120 --> 01:51:14.247
other kinds of
reactions to this stuff?

01:51:14.247 --> 01:51:16.122
AUDIENCE: It's still
kind of surprising to me

01:51:16.122 --> 01:51:18.818
as far as with the
flies [INAUDIBLE]..

01:51:22.188 --> 01:51:23.480
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, it carries.

01:51:23.480 --> 01:51:25.410
It really travels,
right, it carries well.

01:51:25.410 --> 01:51:29.110
Yeah, especially the double
shot, with the flies.

01:51:29.110 --> 01:51:30.710
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:51:30.710 --> 01:51:34.800
Any other reactions,
thoughts on this?

01:51:34.800 --> 01:51:38.660
So what did some of these early
films then bring together?

01:51:38.660 --> 01:51:41.180
The popular interest in
science and natural history,

01:51:41.180 --> 01:51:44.700
along with the interest in
science, at the same time,

01:51:44.700 --> 01:51:50.983
so bringing together both
in the same domain or space.

01:51:50.983 --> 01:51:52.400
The interest in
spectacle, so this

01:51:52.400 --> 01:51:55.160
is part of what Chris was
talking about earlier, again,

01:51:55.160 --> 01:51:57.410
these exciting new ways of
seeing the world associated

01:51:57.410 --> 01:51:58.910
with cameras and
what they could do,

01:51:58.910 --> 01:52:02.810
and then also just the sense
of spectacle through that.

01:52:02.810 --> 01:52:04.880
And popular entertainment
as we talked

01:52:04.880 --> 01:52:10.030
about, music acts, vaudeville,
popular entertainment-- so all

01:52:10.030 --> 01:52:11.280
of these coming together here.

01:52:11.280 --> 01:52:13.310
And again, as
Chris mentioned, we

01:52:13.310 --> 01:52:15.800
do see-- it's not completely
foreign that we do see this

01:52:15.800 --> 01:52:18.770
again like the big
science IMAX films,

01:52:18.770 --> 01:52:21.560
right, where it's about the
spectacle of seeing things

01:52:21.560 --> 01:52:24.860
in this way because
they're so huge.

01:52:24.860 --> 01:52:27.680
And you want to sort of
feel the power of it.

01:52:27.680 --> 01:52:30.915
It's presented to the audiences
that this is about science.

01:52:30.915 --> 01:52:33.290
And this is at least somewhat
educational, good for kids.

01:52:33.290 --> 01:52:34.790
But it's also very
much entertainment.

01:52:34.790 --> 01:52:37.160
And so again, where's the
boundary between entertainment

01:52:37.160 --> 01:52:42.230
and science and
spectacle and looking

01:52:42.230 --> 01:52:46.030
at the world in new scientific
ways or whatever it might be.

01:52:46.030 --> 01:52:47.780
So the point of seeing
these early science

01:52:47.780 --> 01:52:49.890
films, and a couple of
things we want to emphasize.

01:52:49.890 --> 01:52:51.848
Number one is that the
science films were there

01:52:51.848 --> 01:52:53.570
from the very
beginning of cinema.

01:52:53.570 --> 01:52:56.450
So science films were
there from the start.

01:52:56.450 --> 01:52:58.820
That these films were
always in conversation

01:52:58.820 --> 01:53:00.200
with other forms
of entertainment

01:53:00.200 --> 01:53:01.547
and popular culture.

01:53:01.547 --> 01:53:03.630
So now it might be in
conversation with Hollywood,

01:53:03.630 --> 01:53:04.130
for example.

01:53:04.130 --> 01:53:05.505
Back then, it was
in conversation

01:53:05.505 --> 01:53:07.710
with vaudeville, music
shows, whatever it might be.

01:53:07.710 --> 01:53:09.668
But that there was always
some kind of dialogue

01:53:09.668 --> 01:53:10.800
back and forth.

01:53:10.800 --> 01:53:13.640
And again, this ongoing
both link and tension

01:53:13.640 --> 01:53:16.430
between science films as a form
of education and science films

01:53:16.430 --> 01:53:17.900
as a form of entertainment.

01:53:17.900 --> 01:53:20.180
And where do you
draw a boundary?

01:53:20.180 --> 01:53:21.380
Is there a boundary?

01:53:21.380 --> 01:53:23.838
Once a boundary starts
to be differentiated,

01:53:23.838 --> 01:53:25.505
where's the appropriate
boundary or not?

01:53:28.838 --> 01:53:30.380
And that's the end
of my bit on that.

01:53:30.380 --> 01:53:34.700
Any further thoughts,
reactions to these early films?

01:53:34.700 --> 01:53:39.150
Have guys ever seen any of these
before from this time period?

01:53:39.150 --> 01:53:39.650
No?

01:53:42.558 --> 01:53:43.100
I don't know.

01:53:43.100 --> 01:53:44.055
It's cool.

01:53:44.055 --> 01:53:44.930
I think they're cool.

01:53:44.930 --> 01:53:47.570
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
they're great.

01:53:47.570 --> 01:53:51.620
Especially the Lumiere
shorts and the juggling fly.

01:53:51.620 --> 01:53:53.000
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:53:53.000 --> 01:53:55.498
Anyway, and you can find,
if you're interested,

01:53:55.498 --> 01:53:56.790
you can find more stuff online.

01:53:56.790 --> 01:53:57.980
And we also have--

01:53:57.980 --> 01:54:02.310
one thing that in
the DV lab office,

01:54:02.310 --> 01:54:06.318
we also have the DVDs of
stuff that we're showing

01:54:06.318 --> 01:54:07.610
and others that you can borrow.

01:54:07.610 --> 01:54:09.320
A lot of them, there's
three copies of things,

01:54:09.320 --> 01:54:11.090
if multiple students
want to borrow stuff.

01:54:11.090 --> 01:54:12.020
This one we only have one.

01:54:12.020 --> 01:54:13.290
But if you're interested in--

01:54:13.290 --> 01:54:14.790
you can also sign
them out with Dave

01:54:14.790 --> 01:54:16.778
if you're interested
in watching more.

01:54:16.778 --> 01:54:19.070
But unless you guys have any
further thoughts, comments

01:54:19.070 --> 01:54:20.487
on this, we'll go
right to talking

01:54:20.487 --> 01:54:22.985
about the logistics of things
and looking at the syllabus.

01:54:27.140 --> 01:54:30.950
And Dave, feel free to
throw in any thoughts

01:54:30.950 --> 01:54:34.130
if you think of anything.

01:54:34.130 --> 01:54:35.240
OK, what big things?

01:54:35.240 --> 01:54:37.365
We already talked about
how the class is divided up

01:54:37.365 --> 01:54:41.390
into Wednesday,
Friday, and how, again,

01:54:41.390 --> 01:54:43.370
that we've tried to-- we
don't want things to--

01:54:43.370 --> 01:54:46.288
there's a lot of material-- but
we don't want things to seem--

01:54:46.288 --> 01:54:48.080
we want things to seem
relatively contained

01:54:48.080 --> 01:54:52.830
and we don't want it to
be too much for people.

01:54:52.830 --> 01:54:55.580
So again, that we
tried very hard to have

01:54:55.580 --> 01:54:57.770
the articles that
you're going to read

01:54:57.770 --> 01:54:59.390
be quite short and concise.

01:54:59.390 --> 01:55:01.010
There's one short paper.

01:55:01.010 --> 01:55:01.790
It's quite short.

01:55:01.790 --> 01:55:02.910
It's early on.

01:55:02.910 --> 01:55:04.712
And the reason why we
did that is because

01:55:04.712 --> 01:55:06.170
towards the beginning,
you're going

01:55:06.170 --> 01:55:08.720
to be doing less
production work as you just

01:55:08.720 --> 01:55:10.650
learn how to do
that use the camera

01:55:10.650 --> 01:55:11.780
and learn how to use sound.

01:55:11.780 --> 01:55:14.310
And so we do the paper early on.

01:55:14.310 --> 01:55:15.920
And then later,
the focus is more

01:55:15.920 --> 01:55:17.970
on the visual end of things.

01:55:17.970 --> 01:55:22.190
And we do want people
to do a final project.

01:55:22.190 --> 01:55:24.632
And the idea, again,
is linking together

01:55:24.632 --> 01:55:26.840
to thinking about documentary
filmmaking as a process

01:55:26.840 --> 01:55:27.915
and science as a process.

01:55:27.915 --> 01:55:30.290
So we want to encourage people
to find some kind of topic

01:55:30.290 --> 01:55:32.660
that's broadly related to
science and engineering,

01:55:32.660 --> 01:55:34.880
but can be very loose.

01:55:34.880 --> 01:55:37.010
Come up with what you will.

01:55:37.010 --> 01:55:40.935
But at least that's
somewhat the idea.

01:55:40.935 --> 01:55:42.560
And particularly,
because we're at MIT,

01:55:42.560 --> 01:55:44.420
there's a wealth
of topics around.

01:55:44.420 --> 01:55:46.310
And towards the end, we
can talk a little bit

01:55:46.310 --> 01:55:49.220
about some of the topics
that students have made films

01:55:49.220 --> 01:55:51.680
on in the past and
ideas that there might

01:55:51.680 --> 01:55:53.405
be things to do around campus.

01:55:53.405 --> 01:55:56.030
CHRIS BOEBEL: And this should be
a good time, again, to mention

01:55:56.030 --> 01:55:58.977
that next Friday, February 11--

01:55:58.977 --> 01:56:00.560
do we have enough
to pass this around?

01:56:00.560 --> 01:56:02.270
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, we've
got-- well, we've got three,

01:56:02.270 --> 01:56:02.930
so at least--

01:56:02.930 --> 01:56:04.083
if anybody wants--

01:56:04.083 --> 01:56:05.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: At the
MIT Museum, there's

01:56:05.750 --> 01:56:11.090
going to be a screening of
last year's Dv lab projects.

01:56:11.090 --> 01:56:13.407
And I would definitely,
if you are available,

01:56:13.407 --> 01:56:14.990
if you can make it,
I would definitely

01:56:14.990 --> 01:56:17.010
encourage you to come.

01:56:17.010 --> 01:56:21.567
It's a really wide range of
films on many, many topics.

01:56:21.567 --> 01:56:23.900
And as Chris said, science
and engineering, very broadly

01:56:23.900 --> 01:56:26.330
defined.

01:56:26.330 --> 01:56:28.310
So there's a lot of
really interesting stuff.

01:56:28.310 --> 01:56:32.480
And it'll give you a sense
of what other people have

01:56:32.480 --> 01:56:34.850
done in this class.

01:56:34.850 --> 01:56:40.790
Everybody last year came in with
basically no experience at all.

01:56:40.790 --> 01:56:43.765
And they produced some really,
really interesting work.

01:56:43.765 --> 01:56:45.890
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And the
final projects are short.

01:56:45.890 --> 01:56:49.080
So they range from say
two minutes to 12 minutes.

01:56:49.080 --> 01:56:51.740
So they're not--
we're not expecting

01:56:51.740 --> 01:56:52.880
a documentary at the end.

01:56:52.880 --> 01:56:55.323
And also what the
students from last--

01:56:55.323 --> 01:56:56.990
the class was slightly
bigger than this,

01:56:56.990 --> 01:56:58.407
but it was still
relatively small.

01:56:58.407 --> 01:56:59.870
I think we had eight students.

01:56:59.870 --> 01:57:04.460
And seven students
kind of voluntarily--

01:57:04.460 --> 01:57:07.887
they wanted to take another
semester of the class.

01:57:07.887 --> 01:57:09.470
And we didn't have
an advanced version

01:57:09.470 --> 01:57:10.550
of this class,
which we're trying

01:57:10.550 --> 01:57:13.470
to work on for students who want
to keep going further with it.

01:57:13.470 --> 01:57:16.490
And so they kind of all did
it as an independent reading

01:57:16.490 --> 01:57:17.180
group.

01:57:17.180 --> 01:57:18.955
So I mean--

01:57:18.955 --> 01:57:20.330
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Right, so actually,

01:57:20.330 --> 01:57:21.950
the films that you
would see next week

01:57:21.950 --> 01:57:24.860
are more evolved
versions of what

01:57:24.860 --> 01:57:27.500
they turned in at the end
of the semester last spring.

01:57:27.500 --> 01:57:30.080
They spent another
semester working on them

01:57:30.080 --> 01:57:33.140
at a less intensive
level, but they

01:57:33.140 --> 01:57:35.600
did do a lot more editing
in some cases more shooting.

01:57:35.600 --> 01:57:36.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY: --through
a second semester.

01:57:36.960 --> 01:57:39.710
But it was nice to know that
nearly every single student who

01:57:39.710 --> 01:57:42.800
took the class was so interested
and excited in the topic

01:57:42.800 --> 01:57:48.278
that they chose to take a
whole semester, working on a--

01:57:48.278 --> 01:57:49.820
CHRIS BOEBEL: --less
intensive level.

01:57:52.580 --> 01:57:54.447
And they did a lot
of really fun work.

01:57:54.447 --> 01:57:56.780
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And just
mention, since we're on this,

01:57:56.780 --> 01:57:58.988
we might as well mention
right now some of the topics

01:57:58.988 --> 01:58:00.650
that they did.

01:58:00.650 --> 01:58:02.900
One of them, one
student did one,

01:58:02.900 --> 01:58:06.830
she wanted to convey how the
repetitive nature of lab work.

01:58:06.830 --> 01:58:09.650
So how do you convey the
competitive nature of lab work

01:58:09.650 --> 01:58:12.030
without really
boring your audience?

01:58:12.030 --> 01:58:15.200
So she kind of cut it in a
really fun, fast, quick way.

01:58:15.200 --> 01:58:20.120
But the whole idea is to
convey repetitive tasks

01:58:20.120 --> 01:58:21.275
in the laboratory.

01:58:21.275 --> 01:58:22.650
And it's like a
two-minute piece.

01:58:22.650 --> 01:58:23.180
And it's really fun.

01:58:23.180 --> 01:58:24.200
And it's nice to watch.

01:58:27.050 --> 01:58:30.172
Totally different topic,
another student ended up doing--

01:58:30.172 --> 01:58:31.880
this is technology
very loosely defined--

01:58:31.880 --> 01:58:34.550
she ended up doing a piece
about Armenian puppet theater.

01:58:34.550 --> 01:58:40.520
She ended up going to
Armenia over the summer.

01:58:40.520 --> 01:58:41.700
She got some money to do it.

01:58:41.700 --> 01:58:44.270
And she ended up-- she
and a partner of hers

01:58:44.270 --> 01:58:47.543
ended up filming this
really beautiful footage

01:58:47.543 --> 01:58:48.960
of this puppet
theater in Armenia.

01:58:48.960 --> 01:58:52.190
So she came back and
made a piece about that.

01:58:52.190 --> 01:58:53.900
We had a Wellesley
student who ended up

01:58:53.900 --> 01:58:58.385
doing what it was called an
edible garden at Wellesley.

01:58:58.385 --> 01:59:00.260
So it was thinking about
environmental issues

01:59:00.260 --> 01:59:02.400
that, so science, that end.

01:59:02.400 --> 01:59:04.220
One of the other
graduate students

01:59:04.220 --> 01:59:06.810
was following how trash
circulated on campus,

01:59:06.810 --> 01:59:12.680
so from outside her dorm door,
to following it into the--

01:59:12.680 --> 01:59:16.058
following its way along campus.

01:59:16.058 --> 01:59:17.600
I'm not even sure
where she ended it,

01:59:17.600 --> 01:59:19.017
because she was
still finishing it

01:59:19.017 --> 01:59:22.917
up quickly before the
screening next week.

01:59:22.917 --> 01:59:24.750
Tom-- did you want to
describe Tom's about--

01:59:24.750 --> 01:59:26.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: So another
grad student in STS

01:59:26.970 --> 01:59:31.980
did a film about a controversy
in a materials science lab

01:59:31.980 --> 01:59:39.030
over how to interpret images
from atomic force microscopy

01:59:39.030 --> 01:59:40.590
instrumentation.

01:59:40.590 --> 01:59:43.500
So this is imaging
extremely small,

01:59:43.500 --> 01:59:46.530
nanoscale structures,
things that

01:59:46.530 --> 01:59:48.900
are so small that actually
the instruments are

01:59:48.900 --> 01:59:49.860
doing the interpreting.

01:59:49.860 --> 01:59:53.430
And you can't actually take an
actual picture of something.

01:59:53.430 --> 01:59:56.430
You're only getting an
interpolated version

01:59:56.430 --> 01:59:58.080
of what's there.

01:59:58.080 --> 02:00:00.780
And of course, so in that
case, you really you're

02:00:00.780 --> 02:00:03.690
interpreting the image, just
like actually in a lot of ways,

02:00:03.690 --> 02:00:04.620
watching a film.

02:00:04.620 --> 02:00:07.080
So it created this
controversy within this lab,

02:00:07.080 --> 02:00:10.290
with different people having
different interpretations,

02:00:10.290 --> 02:00:11.850
which actually
turned quite serious.

02:00:11.850 --> 02:00:13.350
And he did a film about that.

02:00:13.350 --> 02:00:14.972
It was a really
interesting film.

02:00:14.972 --> 02:00:16.680
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
Max, undergraduate,

02:00:16.680 --> 02:00:19.410
did a short piece
that's a portrait

02:00:19.410 --> 02:00:24.030
of a graduate student
who works in a lab, who's

02:00:24.030 --> 02:00:25.710
also a stand up comic.

02:00:25.710 --> 02:00:28.190
So and this was actually--
his earlier project had been--

02:00:28.190 --> 02:00:30.690
and I'm actually sorry he didn't
finish the earlier project,

02:00:30.690 --> 02:00:33.150
because the earlier the
earlier project was really fun.

02:00:33.150 --> 02:00:34.020
This one's fun too.

02:00:34.020 --> 02:00:35.700
But the earlier
one was he wanted

02:00:35.700 --> 02:00:41.790
to work against images of
science students as geeks.

02:00:41.790 --> 02:00:44.760
And so he had this
really funny introduction

02:00:44.760 --> 02:00:47.100
where he went around and he
just did random interviews

02:00:47.100 --> 02:00:48.850
with people around
Cambridge and in Boston

02:00:48.850 --> 02:00:52.708
about what do you think about
scientists and MIT students.

02:00:52.708 --> 02:00:54.750
You get all these like
stereotypical stereotypes.

02:00:54.750 --> 02:00:56.792
And then he would kind of
went around campus here

02:00:56.792 --> 02:00:59.010
and tried to counter
that, but in the very kind

02:00:59.010 --> 02:01:01.030
of funny, humorous way.

02:01:01.030 --> 02:01:02.700
So that was a fun piece.

02:01:02.700 --> 02:01:06.810
So he's got the short
portrait of this scientist

02:01:06.810 --> 02:01:10.950
slash stand-up comic that
he's going to be showing.

02:01:10.950 --> 02:01:11.823
What else?

02:01:11.823 --> 02:01:12.490
I'm spacing out.

02:01:12.490 --> 02:01:14.610
Melissa's on West
African cloth--

02:01:14.610 --> 02:01:16.110
she actually
originally started out

02:01:16.110 --> 02:01:21.570
doing one about the way
Africa was being represented

02:01:21.570 --> 02:01:23.790
in discussions about
international development

02:01:23.790 --> 02:01:25.315
around campus at MIT.

02:01:25.315 --> 02:01:27.440
So that was the original
project she started doing.

02:01:27.440 --> 02:01:30.755
And then the second one ended up
being about West African cloth.

02:01:30.755 --> 02:01:32.130
So again, you
could think of that

02:01:32.130 --> 02:01:33.525
as technology broadly defined.

02:01:36.930 --> 02:01:39.570
[? Janai's ?] earlier
piece was on the Fab Lab.

02:01:39.570 --> 02:01:41.010
She interviewed a
graduate student

02:01:41.010 --> 02:01:46.130
who is working on
prosthetic pieces in India,

02:01:46.130 --> 02:01:47.880
but focusing on this
person in their labs,

02:01:47.880 --> 02:01:49.890
so kind of a portrait
a person in the lab.

02:01:49.890 --> 02:01:50.910
But what else?

02:01:50.910 --> 02:01:54.210
CHRIS BOEBEL: Emily,
another grad student,

02:01:54.210 --> 02:01:59.910
did a film about livestock
competitions at a state fair.

02:01:59.910 --> 02:02:03.780
And it's got a lot of very
interesting kind of look

02:02:03.780 --> 02:02:07.080
at competitiveness and
human-animal relationships.

02:02:07.080 --> 02:02:10.830
And again, it has a very
science technology and society

02:02:10.830 --> 02:02:11.780
approach.

02:02:11.780 --> 02:02:13.530
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
environmental also--

02:02:13.530 --> 02:02:18.300
CHRIS BOEBEL: And environmental
approach to this event.

02:02:18.300 --> 02:02:20.970
So anyway, it definitely
are worth checking out.

02:02:20.970 --> 02:02:25.260
It might be fun for you
to see what they produced.

02:02:25.260 --> 02:02:29.010
But one thing that we should
also say on the other side,

02:02:29.010 --> 02:02:33.982
is we fully recognize that
this is a difficult process.

02:02:33.982 --> 02:02:36.190
And as I said before, there's
a steep learning curve.

02:02:36.190 --> 02:02:40.900
So there's a lot of
resources to help you.

02:02:40.900 --> 02:02:42.870
There's the lab, there's
Dave, there's me,

02:02:42.870 --> 02:02:46.320
there are office
hours appointments.

02:02:46.320 --> 02:02:51.210
We will do our best to help
you through the process.

02:02:51.210 --> 02:02:53.580
And it's really absorbing.

02:02:53.580 --> 02:02:55.770
Almost everyone gets really
interested in engaged

02:02:55.770 --> 02:02:57.460
with what they're doing.

02:02:57.460 --> 02:02:59.670
But there are those
disasters that happen.

02:02:59.670 --> 02:03:04.320
I mean films don't work
out, either by accident

02:03:04.320 --> 02:03:07.800
or just bad luck.

02:03:07.800 --> 02:03:10.590
Cameras break, or
footage doesn't turn out.

02:03:10.590 --> 02:03:13.830
Or what do you?

02:03:13.830 --> 02:03:15.900
But there is a bail.

02:03:15.900 --> 02:03:17.910
There is a way to bail.

02:03:17.910 --> 02:03:21.480
And that is-- we
strongly discourage it,

02:03:21.480 --> 02:03:29.412
but there is-- you can write a
paper about why your film isn't

02:03:29.412 --> 02:03:30.370
what you want it to be.

02:03:30.370 --> 02:03:32.970
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
and this is described--

02:03:32.970 --> 02:03:35.670
I believe in the syllabus, we
have a description of this.

02:03:35.670 --> 02:03:39.720
So yeah, not to put so
much pressure on this--

02:03:39.720 --> 02:03:41.370
I mean particularly,
a lot of what

02:03:41.370 --> 02:03:42.953
you're going be doing
early on is just

02:03:42.953 --> 02:03:45.750
individual exercises as practice
to get to know how to use

02:03:45.750 --> 02:03:47.550
the camera and the equipment.

02:03:47.550 --> 02:03:50.740
And then so then this is
largely this final project.

02:03:50.740 --> 02:03:54.300
So the idea is, again, because
people, a lot of people

02:03:54.300 --> 02:03:56.708
will not be having
any prior experience,

02:03:56.708 --> 02:03:59.250
that if the video-- and this is
part of the learning process,

02:03:59.250 --> 02:04:01.083
right-- if it doesn't
come out quite the way

02:04:01.083 --> 02:04:02.850
that you want it
to be, that's just

02:04:02.850 --> 02:04:04.830
part of the learning process.

02:04:04.830 --> 02:04:06.450
So what we wanted
to do, so people

02:04:06.450 --> 02:04:09.390
don't have to worry
about grades or other--

02:04:09.390 --> 02:04:11.160
because again, it's
part of the process.

02:04:11.160 --> 02:04:13.140
You don't want to feel bad if it
doesn't come out quite the way

02:04:13.140 --> 02:04:14.500
that you wanted it to come out.

02:04:14.500 --> 02:04:15.998
So what we wanted--

02:04:15.998 --> 02:04:17.790
so what we do is we
give people the option.

02:04:17.790 --> 02:04:20.760
Then if you don't like the way
your final project came out,

02:04:20.760 --> 02:04:26.030
you can submit it along
with a five-page paper where

02:04:26.030 --> 02:04:30.200
you talk about what
you were trying to do,

02:04:30.200 --> 02:04:32.990
and why it didn't quite
end up being the way

02:04:32.990 --> 02:04:34.150
that you wanted it to do.

02:04:34.150 --> 02:04:35.900
And we'll grade you
on and we'll grade you

02:04:35.900 --> 02:04:37.830
on that and the amount of
effort you put into it.

02:04:37.830 --> 02:04:39.110
CHRIS BOEBEL: So it's
not instead of a film,

02:04:39.110 --> 02:04:40.152
it's along with the film.

02:04:40.152 --> 02:04:43.610
It's kind of get to write a
response to your own film.

02:04:43.610 --> 02:04:47.683
If it's not-- if you
feel like uncomfortable

02:04:47.683 --> 02:04:49.100
that something
happened and you're

02:04:49.100 --> 02:04:50.470
afraid of being graded on it.

02:04:50.470 --> 02:04:52.220
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
ultimately, the grade

02:04:52.220 --> 02:04:54.510
is not-- we're not going
to grade-- because I mean,

02:04:54.510 --> 02:04:56.630
this is not the
case in this class,

02:04:56.630 --> 02:04:58.815
but it could conceivably
be the case in the class

02:04:58.815 --> 02:05:00.440
where somebody has
a lot of experience.

02:05:00.440 --> 02:05:01.760
And then you have other
students who have none.

02:05:01.760 --> 02:05:04.590
And you can't compare the
outcomes as being the same.

02:05:04.590 --> 02:05:07.310
So the focus in terms of
grading for that final project

02:05:07.310 --> 02:05:09.140
is largely the amount
of effort that you're

02:05:09.140 --> 02:05:10.410
going to put into it.

02:05:10.410 --> 02:05:13.970
So again, it's all part
of a learning process.

02:05:13.970 --> 02:05:16.790
And we don't expect things to be
perfect coming out at the end.

02:05:16.790 --> 02:05:18.980
So don't freak out about that.

02:05:18.980 --> 02:05:21.320
And you're going to be graded
on the amount of effort

02:05:21.320 --> 02:05:23.760
that you put into to try
and put your piece together.

02:05:23.760 --> 02:05:25.490
MAN OFFSCREEN: One thing I
would encourage you guys to do

02:05:25.490 --> 02:05:27.965
is to get to know each
other, like exchange numbers

02:05:27.965 --> 02:05:31.840
and contact info, because a
lot of these projects, really

02:05:31.840 --> 02:05:34.300
helps to have an
extra set of hands.

02:05:34.300 --> 02:05:35.674
[INAUDIBLE] same thing.

02:05:37.377 --> 02:05:39.710
CHRIS BOEBEL: And I think
we'll actually formalize that.

02:05:39.710 --> 02:05:43.370
I mean obviously if people
have concerns about sharing

02:05:43.370 --> 02:05:44.480
their email, that's fine.

02:05:44.480 --> 02:05:45.590
You can opt out.

02:05:45.590 --> 02:05:49.730
But we will I think,
probably even on Friday,

02:05:49.730 --> 02:05:53.240
create a mailing list
and have everybody

02:05:53.240 --> 02:05:56.690
have everyone else's email
address, because you'll all

02:05:56.690 --> 02:05:59.090
be in a situation where
you'll need somebody

02:05:59.090 --> 02:06:04.580
to help you do sound or help you
carry the equipment to a taxi

02:06:04.580 --> 02:06:06.200
or shoot something.

02:06:06.200 --> 02:06:09.110
And those favors get returned.

02:06:09.110 --> 02:06:11.870
Again, what happens
with these classes

02:06:11.870 --> 02:06:15.140
is that you become a small
community of filmmakers

02:06:15.140 --> 02:06:16.860
and you work together.

02:06:16.860 --> 02:06:18.510
You end up supporting
each other.

02:06:18.510 --> 02:06:21.080
And it's a very important
part of the process.

02:06:21.080 --> 02:06:22.310
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And those
kinds of things, though,

02:06:22.310 --> 02:06:23.750
will be more like the
second half of the class

02:06:23.750 --> 02:06:26.042
when you're working on your
final project, where again,

02:06:26.042 --> 02:06:28.770
the first half of the class is
more these smaller exercises.

02:06:28.770 --> 02:06:30.520
CHRIS BOEBEL: If you
look at the syllabus,

02:06:30.520 --> 02:06:33.200
you'll see that really the
last five or six weeks,

02:06:33.200 --> 02:06:35.930
it really starts to be all
about the final project.

02:06:35.930 --> 02:06:38.510
And a lot of the
reading drops away.

02:06:38.510 --> 02:06:40.430
And a lot of the exercises stop.

02:06:40.430 --> 02:06:44.420
And you're really
given a lot of time

02:06:44.420 --> 02:06:49.850
to develop, conceptualize, and
shoot and edit your project.

02:06:49.850 --> 02:06:52.310
And it starts fairly early.

02:06:52.310 --> 02:06:55.250
We have a formal process where
you think about your ideas.

02:06:55.250 --> 02:06:56.540
You present your ideas.

02:06:56.540 --> 02:06:58.310
You develop your ideas.

02:06:58.310 --> 02:07:01.350
And then you begin
to create them.

02:07:01.350 --> 02:07:04.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So to
talk a bit about equipment,

02:07:04.130 --> 02:07:09.420
and I'm sure Dave is going
to have more to add to this--

02:07:09.420 --> 02:07:12.510
so basically what we have,
we have six camera packages.

02:07:12.510 --> 02:07:14.570
So we have six
high-def cameras--

02:07:14.570 --> 02:07:16.050
or maybe, I don't know maybe
you want to talk about this.

02:07:16.050 --> 02:07:17.342
I don't know if I should go on.

02:07:17.342 --> 02:07:19.790
We have six tripods.

02:07:19.790 --> 02:07:22.640
We've got six sets
of sound equipment.

02:07:22.640 --> 02:07:25.910
There's two nice, big Macs
with Final Cut Pro in there.

02:07:25.910 --> 02:07:29.380
And again, there's all the
Macintosh editing computers

02:07:29.380 --> 02:07:30.380
in the new media center.

02:07:30.380 --> 02:07:32.490
So the information on
all that is in there.

02:07:32.490 --> 02:07:35.000
So that's the equipment that
we have available for you

02:07:35.000 --> 02:07:36.710
guys to use.

02:07:36.710 --> 02:07:37.424
Sorry?

02:07:37.424 --> 02:07:39.080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:07:39.080 --> 02:07:40.538
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Oh, that's right,

02:07:40.538 --> 02:07:42.370
two [? flex films ?]
for a bit later.

02:07:42.370 --> 02:07:47.440
And so bit by bit, again, we're
not expecting any background

02:07:47.440 --> 02:07:47.940
knowledge.

02:07:47.940 --> 02:07:49.670
So on Friday, the
first lab is just

02:07:49.670 --> 02:07:51.980
going to be the basics of
how you shoot the camera,

02:07:51.980 --> 02:07:53.970
I mean just starting
completely at the basics.

02:07:53.970 --> 02:07:55.230
This how you set up a tripod.

02:07:55.230 --> 02:07:56.563
This is how you work the camera.

02:07:56.563 --> 02:07:59.450
The next class will be the very
basics of doing some sound.

02:07:59.450 --> 02:08:02.160
So it just starts
out bit by bit.

02:08:02.160 --> 02:08:04.760
And we do have
optional readings.

02:08:04.760 --> 02:08:06.860
And again, they're
completely optional.

02:08:06.860 --> 02:08:10.140
But if anybody's interested, say
if we're doing sound that week,

02:08:10.140 --> 02:08:13.610
and you want to know
more about sound,

02:08:13.610 --> 02:08:16.550
there's a book that's
a reference book.

02:08:16.550 --> 02:08:18.470
It's one of the--

02:08:18.470 --> 02:08:20.580
we have a copy of
it in the DV lab,

02:08:20.580 --> 02:08:21.830
so you can access it that way.

02:08:21.830 --> 02:08:23.747
But I would encourage
you to have a copy of it

02:08:23.747 --> 02:08:26.840
so you have it around.

02:08:26.840 --> 02:08:30.640
But we'll have recommended
chapters, for example,

02:08:30.640 --> 02:08:33.447
from this one, The
Filmmakers Handbook,

02:08:33.447 --> 02:08:35.780
that can tell you a little
bit more background on sound.

02:08:35.780 --> 02:08:37.970
So you're not meant to
actually read those things.

02:08:37.970 --> 02:08:41.510
I mean you're meant to
use them as references.

02:08:41.510 --> 02:08:43.110
So use them when you
need to use them.

02:08:43.110 --> 02:08:45.110
And there's also a reference
book on an editing.

02:08:45.110 --> 02:08:48.110
So just know that you have
those resources there.

02:08:48.110 --> 02:08:49.760
And if you are
interested and you

02:08:49.760 --> 02:08:52.300
do want to do a little
reading on sound

02:08:52.300 --> 02:08:54.050
or whatever it is that
strikes your fancy,

02:08:54.050 --> 02:08:55.130
you know where to
go look for it.

02:08:55.130 --> 02:08:57.530
But I imagine most students
will probably just use it

02:08:57.530 --> 02:08:58.550
as references when you need it.

02:08:58.550 --> 02:08:59.967
But it's nice to
have them around.

02:08:59.967 --> 02:09:02.120
CHRIS BOEBEL: The editing
book, in particular,

02:09:02.120 --> 02:09:03.450
it's a very good book.

02:09:03.450 --> 02:09:06.050
It's essentially a software
manual for Final Cut Pro.

02:09:06.050 --> 02:09:09.800
And I would strongly
recommend that you buy it

02:09:09.800 --> 02:09:11.430
or get access to it in some way.

02:09:11.430 --> 02:09:13.340
I mean there's also a
lot of online material.

02:09:13.340 --> 02:09:18.710
But a lot of editing is
learning how to edit and think

02:09:18.710 --> 02:09:19.580
like an editor.

02:09:19.580 --> 02:09:22.160
But there's also a whole
level of software training

02:09:22.160 --> 02:09:26.460
that we will spend some time on
here, but honestly not enough,

02:09:26.460 --> 02:09:28.560
just because we don't
have enough time to do it.

02:09:28.560 --> 02:09:33.050
So you will definitely
want to seek out

02:09:33.050 --> 02:09:34.640
other resources for editing.

02:09:34.640 --> 02:09:36.950
And again, that includes
Dave, that includes me,

02:09:36.950 --> 02:09:41.210
that includes this manual,
or online resources.

02:09:41.210 --> 02:09:44.172
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And there is
also a copy of that book also.

02:09:44.172 --> 02:09:46.130
MAN OFFSCREEN: [INAUDIBLE]
just give a website.

02:09:46.130 --> 02:09:48.710
I heard that
[INAUDIBLE] students

02:09:48.710 --> 02:09:52.710
get free access to the
online tutorials, lynda.com.

02:09:52.710 --> 02:09:56.192
It's L-Y-N-D-A .com.

02:09:56.192 --> 02:09:57.650
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right,
it's a great--

02:09:57.650 --> 02:09:59.542
MAN OFFSCREEN: L-Y-N-D-A .com.

02:09:59.542 --> 02:10:01.250
CHRIS BOEBEL: A great
resource, and yeah,

02:10:01.250 --> 02:10:04.760
MIT just cut a deal that
allows MIT students to get

02:10:04.760 --> 02:10:05.492
free access.

02:10:05.492 --> 02:10:06.950
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But again, that's

02:10:06.950 --> 02:10:08.715
a resource you're
not expected to do.

02:10:08.715 --> 02:10:11.090
I mean, this is if you decide
your interest in this stuff

02:10:11.090 --> 02:10:12.673
and you're excited
and you want to put

02:10:12.673 --> 02:10:15.330
the extra time into doing, it
all those resources are there.

02:10:15.330 --> 02:10:19.100
So that's one thing that is
really exciting about being

02:10:19.100 --> 02:10:20.060
able to do this class.

02:10:20.060 --> 02:10:22.227
And we're very grateful for
the support we've gotten

02:10:22.227 --> 02:10:25.430
and doing it, is because
there's a lot of resources

02:10:25.430 --> 02:10:29.420
here for a very small
group of people.

02:10:29.420 --> 02:10:31.040
So you've got all
this equipment.

02:10:31.040 --> 02:10:36.400
And you've got three
people, and just lots

02:10:36.400 --> 02:10:38.650
of resources that are able
to take advantage of if you

02:10:38.650 --> 02:10:39.790
really--

02:10:39.790 --> 02:10:43.610
and people will do this
to different levels.

02:10:43.610 --> 02:10:47.500
Some people will just sort of--

02:10:47.500 --> 02:10:49.220
some people will get
really excited about

02:10:49.220 --> 02:10:50.178
and want to do it more.

02:10:50.178 --> 02:10:52.300
And you've got the resources
if you want to do it.

02:10:52.300 --> 02:10:55.180
And other people will not have
the time to be able to do that.

02:10:55.180 --> 02:10:56.682
And you're not
expected to do that.

02:10:56.682 --> 02:10:58.390
But these are just
the resources that you

02:10:58.390 --> 02:11:00.795
have in case you want to
take advantage of them.

02:11:00.795 --> 02:11:01.420
So that's that.

02:11:01.420 --> 02:11:02.800
But other things
about the equipment--

02:11:02.800 --> 02:11:05.342
one thing about the hard drives,
also-- you're going to get--

02:11:05.342 --> 02:11:09.430
everybody is going to get
a hard drive to put your--

02:11:09.430 --> 02:11:12.538
once you start getting
material, you're

02:11:12.538 --> 02:11:14.080
going to store it
on this hard drive.

02:11:14.080 --> 02:11:15.080
They're really delicate.

02:11:15.080 --> 02:11:17.290
And we've had issues
with some of them smoking

02:11:17.290 --> 02:11:20.130
and bad things happening
to them, because they're

02:11:20.130 --> 02:11:21.880
really not meant to
be-- they're delicate,

02:11:21.880 --> 02:11:24.070
they're not really meant
to be carted around.

02:11:24.070 --> 02:11:26.362
But on the other hand, you
have to bring them to class.

02:11:26.362 --> 02:11:28.063
So just be careful with them.

02:11:28.063 --> 02:11:29.980
Like if you have to put
them in your backpack,

02:11:29.980 --> 02:11:31.090
wrap them in something.

02:11:31.090 --> 02:11:32.935
Or just try to do what you can.

02:11:32.935 --> 02:11:33.190
CHRIS BOEBEL: They
will come with a box.

02:11:33.190 --> 02:11:34.018
You'll have a box.

02:11:34.018 --> 02:11:34.810
But it's a little--

02:11:34.810 --> 02:11:36.430
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Try to be as
gentle with them as possible

02:11:36.430 --> 02:11:38.388
because you don't want
to end up in a situation

02:11:38.388 --> 02:11:41.122
where something happens to the
drive and you lose all your--

02:11:41.122 --> 02:11:42.580
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
just to be clear,

02:11:42.580 --> 02:11:44.505
you'll have the use of
it for the semester.

02:11:44.505 --> 02:11:46.880
But you'll have to turn it in
at the end of the semester.

02:11:46.880 --> 02:11:50.350
So you'll want to think
about ways of packing up

02:11:50.350 --> 02:11:52.750
your project, or at
least the final version,

02:11:52.750 --> 02:11:56.720
on a data disk or another
hard drive or whatever.

02:11:56.720 --> 02:12:00.017
But you do not have to buy
your own hard drive in order

02:12:00.017 --> 02:12:00.850
to be in this class.

02:12:00.850 --> 02:12:02.140
We give you a hard drive.

02:12:02.140 --> 02:12:05.090
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And also
tapes for the cameras--

02:12:05.090 --> 02:12:08.260
everybody can get a couple tapes
out when you go to shoot stuff,

02:12:08.260 --> 02:12:09.160
you can get a tape.

02:12:09.160 --> 02:12:14.080
But if you're using
more than one or two,

02:12:14.080 --> 02:12:16.330
you would be expected to
buy the additional tapes,

02:12:16.330 --> 02:12:18.460
because we don't have
enough money in the budget

02:12:18.460 --> 02:12:19.510
to be able to--

02:12:19.510 --> 02:12:21.790
CHRIS BOEBEL: I
mean, again people

02:12:21.790 --> 02:12:25.510
shoot anywhere from a couple
of one-hour tapes for a project

02:12:25.510 --> 02:12:26.595
to 15.

02:12:26.595 --> 02:12:28.720
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Depending
what you have time for.

02:12:28.720 --> 02:12:30.387
CHRIS BOEBEL: --on
what your ambition is

02:12:30.387 --> 02:12:31.660
and what your project is.

02:12:31.660 --> 02:12:34.300
So we can't give
unlimited tapes.

02:12:34.300 --> 02:12:38.710
But we do have a small number
that we will provide you.

02:12:38.710 --> 02:12:40.010
And they're not that expensive.

02:12:40.010 --> 02:12:42.132
There are only a few apiece.

02:12:42.132 --> 02:12:44.590
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And when you
go to sign the equipment out

02:12:44.590 --> 02:12:47.090
in DV lab, you'll be signing
out the equipment through Dave.

02:12:51.070 --> 02:12:55.690
We do have an insurance
policy on the equipment.

02:12:55.690 --> 02:12:57.790
But it's got a really
high deductible.

02:12:57.790 --> 02:13:00.830
It's like $1,000 deductible.

02:13:00.830 --> 02:13:04.870
So be-- obviously, it
goes without saying,

02:13:04.870 --> 02:13:07.870
be really careful
with the equipment.

02:13:07.870 --> 02:13:11.230
I mean we are covered, again,
with the insurance policy.

02:13:11.230 --> 02:13:16.210
But be as careful as you can.

02:13:16.210 --> 02:13:20.470
Don't lend the equipment
to obviously to other--

02:13:20.470 --> 02:13:24.400
because it's unclear,
legally, whether we

02:13:24.400 --> 02:13:28.900
would be covered if somebody
else who wasn't actively taking

02:13:28.900 --> 02:13:34.240
the class was using
the equipment.

02:13:34.240 --> 02:13:36.880
Anyway, so just be aware
of that with equipment.

02:13:36.880 --> 02:13:38.520
Anything else with
the equipment, Dave?

02:13:38.520 --> 02:13:40.270
MAN OFFSCREEN: I think
it makes more sense

02:13:40.270 --> 02:13:42.440
to talk about it when
we're actually [INAUDIBLE]..

02:13:42.440 --> 02:13:43.100
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, OK.

02:13:43.100 --> 02:13:43.970
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah,
I mean that's just

02:13:43.970 --> 02:13:45.500
that broad overview.

02:13:45.500 --> 02:13:46.760
One other thing--

02:13:46.760 --> 02:13:49.610
Dave and I will both be
setting up our office hours.

02:13:49.610 --> 02:13:54.770
And to the extent that we can,
we'll try to listen to you.

02:13:54.770 --> 02:13:58.070
And since it's a
small class, we'll

02:13:58.070 --> 02:14:02.180
try to set it up at times
that work for as many of you

02:14:02.180 --> 02:14:03.860
as we possibly can accommodate.

02:14:03.860 --> 02:14:06.850
So there's some flexibility
in our schedules.

02:14:06.850 --> 02:14:11.873
If everybody wants us to come in
from 1:30 AM to 3:30 AM Monday,

02:14:11.873 --> 02:14:13.790
Wednesday, Friday, that
probably won't happen.

02:14:13.790 --> 02:14:17.015
But within or to a
reasonable degree,

02:14:17.015 --> 02:14:18.140
we'll try to work with you.

02:14:18.140 --> 02:14:20.425
And we'll go through
all that on Friday.

02:14:20.425 --> 02:14:22.550
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And one
thing, we didn't actually

02:14:22.550 --> 02:14:23.717
talk much about the content.

02:14:23.717 --> 02:14:25.440
We talked about the
logistical issues.

02:14:25.440 --> 02:14:28.280
But in terms of content,
we're starting out

02:14:28.280 --> 02:14:29.930
with a bit of a
chronological, we're

02:14:29.930 --> 02:14:31.100
continuing a bit
chronologically.

02:14:31.100 --> 02:14:32.600
We started with some
of these early films.

02:14:32.600 --> 02:14:35.210
We're going to next week look
at some really interesting ones

02:14:35.210 --> 02:14:37.190
from the 1920s.

02:14:37.190 --> 02:14:43.080
We're going to move up to when
we get to the 1960s, again,

02:14:43.080 --> 02:14:44.760
we have a lot of
changing technology.

02:14:44.760 --> 02:14:47.630
So you start having
handheld cameras

02:14:47.630 --> 02:14:49.400
that also make a
big impact and how

02:14:49.400 --> 02:14:50.810
documentary films can be done.

02:14:53.657 --> 02:14:56.240
So we're going to go through a
number of different documentary

02:14:56.240 --> 02:15:01.250
film trends through the
'60s, through the '80s, '90s.

02:15:03.980 --> 02:15:06.050
We're going to
try to have some--

02:15:06.050 --> 02:15:08.810
it's less of an
international perspective

02:15:08.810 --> 02:15:11.720
than we would like to have if
we had more time in the class.

02:15:11.720 --> 02:15:17.177
But we do try to do some
looks internationally.

02:15:17.177 --> 02:15:19.760
And we're also going to have a
guest speaker this semester who

02:15:19.760 --> 02:15:21.885
does work on documentary
filmmaking in India, who's

02:15:21.885 --> 02:15:23.198
going to be coming to speak.

02:15:23.198 --> 02:15:24.740
And we have a couple
of classes where

02:15:24.740 --> 02:15:25.865
we talk about those issues.

02:15:29.280 --> 02:15:32.775
And then later on it kind
goes through chronologically.

02:15:32.775 --> 02:15:34.400
So you get a sense
of different schools

02:15:34.400 --> 02:15:35.630
of documentary filmmaking.

02:15:35.630 --> 02:15:37.367
And part of the idea
with that is then

02:15:37.367 --> 02:15:39.950
that you get the idea when you
go to do your own piece, here's

02:15:39.950 --> 02:15:41.367
all the different
styles out here.

02:15:41.367 --> 02:15:43.490
I can pick and choose
kind of from what

02:15:43.490 --> 02:15:44.837
I think is interesting to do.

02:15:44.837 --> 02:15:46.670
And then later on, we're
going to focus more

02:15:46.670 --> 02:15:49.280
on particular kind of
topics, like say nature films

02:15:49.280 --> 02:15:51.333
or say science
fiction, which are just

02:15:51.333 --> 02:15:53.000
kind of fun to think
about, and give you

02:15:53.000 --> 02:15:54.667
another sense, again,
of different kinds

02:15:54.667 --> 02:15:57.830
of documentary film work that's
been done out there related

02:15:57.830 --> 02:16:00.450
to science and engineering,
broadly defined.

02:16:00.450 --> 02:16:04.010
So any questions
about any of this?

02:16:04.010 --> 02:16:04.940
No?

02:16:04.940 --> 02:16:07.820
And don't be overwhelmed
by the syllabus.

02:16:07.820 --> 02:16:10.400
This came out of a prior class
that didn't have production.

02:16:10.400 --> 02:16:13.730
But it was like eight
years of teaching it,

02:16:13.730 --> 02:16:16.880
so we've got like, the
syllabus just keeps growing.

02:16:16.880 --> 02:16:18.230
But it's got resources on it.

02:16:18.230 --> 02:16:22.640
And there's a lot of, again,
additional things on the class

02:16:22.640 --> 02:16:25.190
website, just in case
people find them useful.

02:16:25.190 --> 02:16:26.880
Things that have come up and--

02:16:26.880 --> 02:16:29.030
CHRIS BOEBEL: And we'll
have plenty of time

02:16:29.030 --> 02:16:31.850
to talk about some of
the other nuts and bolts

02:16:31.850 --> 02:16:33.142
of the class on Friday as well.

02:16:33.142 --> 02:16:34.642
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, and the lab.

02:16:34.642 --> 02:16:36.423
So the lab meets in
here also on Friday.

02:16:36.423 --> 02:16:38.840
And again, on Friday, we'll
just be doing the introduction

02:16:38.840 --> 02:16:39.423
to the camera.

02:16:42.680 --> 02:16:43.180
That's it.

02:16:43.180 --> 02:16:44.055
CHRIS BOEBEL: Thanks.

02:16:44.055 --> 02:16:45.347
Thanks for coming.

02:16:45.347 --> 02:16:47.139
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Thank
you all for coming.

02:16:47.139 --> 02:16:51.539
Looking forward to having you.

